/* Long-answer bank: Course notes sections 2.1 to 2.4 (examples, exercises and end of section problems).
   Source: D. Wolczuk, Calculus for the Sciences (CC BY-NC 4.0), with the official exercise and problem solutions.
   Section 2.4.5 (exponential growth and decay) was not lectured; those items carry notLectured:true.
   Transcribed from the catalogue in work/notes/coursenotes_ch2a.md. */
M127.addLongAnswer([
  {id:"CN2.1.1-EX1", src:"Course notes 2.1, Example 1", lec:"L06", kind:"example", page:"p. 39",
   q:"When a light switch is off, no electricity flows to the light bulb. Suppose that at time $t=a$ the switch is turned on and 110 V of electricity flows to the bulb. Write a mathematical equation for the voltage supplied to the light bulb.",
   sol:[
    "Before the transition point $t=a$ no electricity flows, and after $t=a$ there are 110 V flowing.",
    "So the voltage is the piecewise defined function $p(t) = \\begin{cases} 0 & \\text{if } t<a \\\\ 110 & \\text{if } t>a \\end{cases}$"],
   ans:"$p(t) = \\begin{cases} 0 & t<a \\\\ 110 & t>a \\end{cases}$"},
  {id:"CN2.1.1-EX2", src:"Course notes 2.1, Example 2", lec:"L06", kind:"example", page:"pp. 39-40",
   q:"In Yellowstone National Park, the number of beaver colonies from 1994 to 1998 was constant at 1. In 1998 wolves were reintroduced, which led to a roughly linear increase in the number of beaver colonies to 19 in 2015. Write a mathematical equation for the number of beaver colonies from 1994 to 2015.",
   sol:[
    "Let $B(t)$ be the number of beaver colonies, where $t$ is the number of years after 1994.",
    "$B(t) = 1$ for $0\\le t\\le 4$, with a transition point at $t=4$ (1998).",
    "For $4 < t \\le 21$, $B$ increases linearly from $B(4)=1$ to $B(21)=19$. Slope: $m = \\dfrac{B(21)-B(4)}{21-4} = \\dfrac{19-1}{17} = \\dfrac{18}{17}$",
    "Point-slope form: $B(t) = 1 + \\frac{18}{17}(t-4)$",
    "So $B(t) = \\begin{cases} 1 & \\text{if } 0\\le t\\le 4 \\\\ 1+\\frac{18}{17}(t-4) & \\text{if } 4 < t \\le 21\\end{cases}$"],
   ans:"$B(t) = \\begin{cases} 1 & 0\\le t\\le 4 \\\\ 1+\\frac{18}{17}(t-4) & 4 < t \\le 21\\end{cases}$, $t$ in years after 1994"},
  {id:"CN2.1.1-XR1", src:"Course notes 2.1, Exercise 1", lec:"L06", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 40",
   q:"The temperature of a room is 20°C. At time $t=0$ the heating system is turned on and the temperature increases by 2°C per hour until it reaches 24°C. At that point the cooling system turns on and the temperature decreases by 1°C per hour until it is back to 20°C. Represent this situation with a piecewise defined function.",
   sol:[
    "Let $T(t)$ be the temperature in °C, $t$ in hours. Starting at $t=0$, $T(t) = 2t+20$.",
    "The temperature reaches 24 when $2t+20=24$, that is after 2 hours. So $T(t) = 2t+20$ for $0\\le t\\le2$.",
    "Cooling at 1 degree per hour takes 4 hours to get back to 20, so for $2<t\\le6$, $T(t) = 24-(t-2)$. (We need $t-2$ because cooling starts at $t=2$.)",
    "$T(t) = \\begin{cases} 2t+20 & \\text{if } 0\\le t\\le2 \\\\ 24-(t-2) & \\text{if } 2<t\\le6\\end{cases}$"],
   ans:"$T(t) = \\begin{cases} 2t+20 & 0\\le t\\le2 \\\\ 24-(t-2) & 2<t\\le6\\end{cases}$"},
  {id:"CN2.1.1-XR3", src:"Course notes 2.1, Exercise 3", lec:"L06", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 40",
   q:"Let $g(x) = \\begin{cases} 3 & \\text{if } x\\le-2 \\\\ -x & \\text{if } -2<x<2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & \\text{if } x\\ge2\\end{cases}$.<br>(a) What is the domain of $g$?<br>(b) What are $g(-2)$, $g(-1)$, $g(1)$, $g(2)$ and $g(3)$?<br>(c) Sketch the graph of $y=g(x)$.",
   sol:[
    "(a) Every real number lies in one of the three pieces, so the domain is $\\mathbb{R}$.",
    "(b) $-2$ is in $x\\le-2$, so $g(-2)=3$.",
    "$-1$ and $1$ are in $-2<x<2$, where $g$ outputs the negative of its input: $g(-1)=1$ and $g(1)=-1$.",
    "$2$ and $3$ are in $x\\ge2$: $g(2)=\\sqrt2$ and $g(3)=\\sqrt3$.",
    "(c) The graph is the horizontal ray $y=3$ for $x\\le-2$ (filled dot at $(-2,3)$), the line $y=-x$ from an open dot at $(-2,2)$ to an open dot at $(2,-2)$, and the curve $y=\\sqrt x$ starting at a filled dot at $(2,\\sqrt2)$ and rising to the right."],
   ans:"(a) $\\mathbb{R}$; (b) $g(-2)=3$, $g(-1)=1$, $g(1)=-1$, $g(2)=\\sqrt2$, $g(3)=\\sqrt3$"},
  {id:"CN2.1.1-XR4", src:"Course notes 2.1, Exercise 4", lec:"L06", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 40",
   q:"Let $A(x) = \\begin{cases} -x & \\text{if } x<0 \\\\ x & \\text{if } x\\ge0\\end{cases}$. Evaluate $A(3)$ and $A(-1)$.",
   sol:[
    "Since $3\\ge0$, $A(3)=3$.",
    "Since $-1<0$, $A(-1) = -(-1) = 1$."],
   ans:"$A(3)=3$, $A(-1)=1$"},
  {id:"CN2.1.2-EX3", src:"Course notes 2.1, Example 3", lec:"L07", kind:"example", page:"p. 41",
   q:"Evaluate $|-3|$ using the definition.",
   sol:[
    "Since $-3<0$, use the first part of the definition: $|-3| = -(-3) = 3$."],
   ans:"$3$"},
  {id:"CN2.1.2-XR5", src:"Course notes 2.1, Exercise 5", lec:"L07", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 41",
   q:"Use the definition to evaluate $|-5|$, $|3|$ and $|-\\sqrt2|$.",
   sol:[
    "Since $-5<0$, $|-5| = -(-5)=5$.",
    "Since $3\\ge0$, $|3|=3$.",
    "Since $-\\sqrt2<0$, $|-\\sqrt2| = -(-\\sqrt2) = \\sqrt2$."],
   ans:"$5$, $3$, $\\sqrt2$"},
  {id:"CN2.1.2-EX4", src:"Course notes 2.1, Example 4", lec:"L07", kind:"example", page:"p. 42",
   q:"Verify that $|x| = \\sqrt{x^2}$ for $x=-2$.",
   sol:[
    "LHS: since $-2<0$, $|-2| = -(-2) = 2$.",
    "RHS: $\\sqrt{(-2)^2} = \\sqrt4 = 2$.",
    "LHS = RHS."],
   ans:"Both sides equal 2."},
  {id:"CN2.1.2-EX5", src:"Course notes 2.1, Example 5", lec:"L07", kind:"example", page:"p. 42",
   q:"Evaluate $\\sqrt{(-2)^{2/3}}$.",
   sol:[
    "By exponent rules, $\\sqrt{(-2)^{2/3}} = \\sqrt{\\left((-2)^{1/3}\\right)^2}$. Do not cancel the square with the square root; use property (i):",
    "$\\sqrt{\\left((-2)^{1/3}\\right)^2} = \\left|(-2)^{1/3}\\right|$",
    "Since $(-2)^{1/3}<0$, by the definition of absolute value this is $-(-2)^{1/3} = 2^{1/3}$."],
   ans:"$2^{1/3}$"},
  {id:"CN2.1.2-EX6", src:"Course notes 2.1, Example 6", lec:"L07", kind:"example", page:"p. 42",
   q:"Find all $x$ satisfying $(x-3)^4 = 5$.",
   sol:[
    "Taking square roots of both sides: $\\sqrt{(x-3)^4} = 5^{1/2}$",
    "$\\left|(x-3)^2\\right| = 5^{1/2}$ by property (i)",
    "$(x-3)^2 = 5^{1/2}$ since $(x-3)^2 \\ge 0$",
    "Taking square roots again: $\\sqrt{(x-3)^2} = 5^{1/4}$, so $|x-3| = 5^{1/4}$ by property (i)",
    "$x-3 = \\pm 5^{1/4}$ by property (iii)",
    "$x = 3 \\pm 5^{1/4}$"],
   ans:"$x = 3\\pm5^{1/4}$",
   note:"The notes justify the third line with $(x-3)^2 > 0$; it should be $\\ge 0$. The notes also print the last line as $x = 3 + \\pm5^{1/4}$."},
  {id:"CN2.1.2-XR6", src:"Course notes 2.1, Exercise 6", lec:"L07", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 43",
   q:"Find all $x$ satisfying $(x-2)^{2/3} = 3$.",
   sol:[
    "Take square roots of both sides: $\\sqrt{(x-2)^{2/3}} = \\sqrt3$",
    "$\\sqrt{\\left((x-2)^{1/3}\\right)^2} = \\sqrt3$, so $\\left|(x-2)^{1/3}\\right| = \\sqrt3$ by property (i)",
    "$(x-2)^{1/3} = \\pm\\sqrt3$ by property (iii)",
    "$x-2 = (\\pm\\sqrt3)^3 = \\pm3^{3/2}$",
    "$x = 2\\pm 3^{3/2}$"],
   ans:"$x = 2\\pm3^{3/2}$"},
  {id:"CN2.1.2-EX7", src:"Course notes 2.1, Example 7", lec:"L07", kind:"example", page:"p. 43",
   q:"Simplify $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x^2+4}}{x}$ when $x<0$.",
   sol:[
    "Factor $x^2$ out of the square root so it can cancel with the $x$ in the denominator:",
    "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x^2+4}}{x} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x^2\\left(1+\\frac{4}{x^2}\\right)}}{x} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x^2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac4{x^2}}}{x} = \\dfrac{|x|\\sqrt{1+\\frac4{x^2}}}{x}$",
    "Since $x<0$, $|x| = -x$: $\\dfrac{-x\\sqrt{1+\\frac4{x^2}}}{x} = -\\sqrt{1+\\dfrac{4}{x^2}}$"],
   ans:"$-\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{x^2}}$"},
  {id:"CN2.1.2-XR7", src:"Course notes 2.1, Exercise 7", lec:"L07", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 43",
   q:"Explain why you do not need absolute value signs when simplifying $\\left(\\sqrt x\\right)^2$.",
   sol:[
    "For $\\sqrt x$ to be a real number, $x$ must already be non-negative. So $\\left(\\sqrt x\\right)^2 = x$ with no absolute value needed."],
   ans:"$x\\ge0$ is already required for $\\sqrt x$ to be defined, so $(\\sqrt x)^2 = x$."},
  {id:"CN2.1.2-XR8", src:"Course notes 2.1, Exercise 8", lec:"L07", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 43",
   q:"Simplify $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2x^2-x}}{x}$ when $x<0$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2x^2-x}}{x} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x^2\\left(2-\\frac1x\\right)}}{x} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x^2}\\sqrt{2-\\frac1x}}{x} = \\dfrac{|x|\\sqrt{2-\\frac1x}}{x}$",
    "Since $x<0$, $|x| = -x$: $\\dfrac{-x\\sqrt{2-\\frac1x}}{x} = -\\sqrt{2-\\dfrac1x}$"],
   ans:"$-\\sqrt{2-\\frac1x}$"},
  {id:"CN2.1.2-EX8", src:"Course notes 2.1, Example 8", lec:"L07", kind:"example", page:"p. 44",
   q:"Find all values of $x$ such that $|x-3|<2$.",
   sol:[
    "By property (iv), $-2 < x-3 < 2$.",
    "Adding 3: $1 < x < 5$."],
   ans:"$1<x<5$"},
  {id:"CN2.1.2-EX9", src:"Course notes 2.1, Example 9", lec:"L07", kind:"example", page:"p. 44",
   q:"Find all values of $x$ such that $|2x+1|<1$.",
   sol:[
    "By property (iv), $-1 < 2x+1 < 1$.",
    "$-2 < 2x < 0$",
    "$-1 < x < 0$"],
   ans:"$-1<x<0$"},
  {id:"CN2.1.2-EX10", src:"Course notes 2.1, Example 10", lec:"L07", kind:"example", page:"p. 44",
   q:"Find all values of $x$ such that $x^2>2$.",
   sol:[
    "Take the square root of both sides: $\\sqrt{x^2} > \\sqrt2$, so $|x| > \\sqrt2$ by property (i).",
    "By property (v), $x<-\\sqrt2$ or $x>\\sqrt2$."],
   ans:"$x<-\\sqrt2$ or $x>\\sqrt2$"},
  {id:"CN2.1.2-XR9", src:"Course notes 2.1, Exercise 9", lec:"L07", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 44",
   q:"Find all values of $x$ satisfying the inequality.<br>(a) $|x+2|<4$<br>(b) $|3x+2|>3$",
   sol:[
    "(a) By property (iv), $-4 < x+2 < 4$, so $-6 < x < 2$.",
    "(b) By property (v), $3x+2<-3$ or $3x+2>3$.",
    "So $3x<-5$ or $3x>1$, that is $x<-\\frac53$ or $x>\\frac13$."],
   ans:"(a) $-6<x<2$; (b) $x<-\\frac53$ or $x>\\frac13$"},
  {id:"CN2.1.2-EX11", src:"Course notes 2.1, Example 11", lec:"L07", kind:"example", page:"p. 45",
   q:"Find all values of $x$ such that $3 < x^2 \\le 5$.",
   sol:[
    "Take square roots: $\\sqrt3 < \\sqrt{x^2} \\le \\sqrt5$, so $\\sqrt3 < |x| \\le \\sqrt5$.",
    "There is no restriction on $x$, so test both cases.",
    "Case 1 ($x<0$): $|x|=-x$, so $\\sqrt3 < -x \\le \\sqrt5$, which gives $-\\sqrt3 > x \\ge -\\sqrt5$.",
    "Case 2 ($x\\ge0$): $|x|=x$, so $\\sqrt3 < x \\le \\sqrt5$.",
    "The solutions are $-\\sqrt5 \\le x < -\\sqrt3$ and $\\sqrt3 < x \\le \\sqrt5$."],
   ans:"$-\\sqrt5 \\le x < -\\sqrt3$ or $\\sqrt3 < x \\le \\sqrt5$"},
  {id:"CN2.1.2-EX12", src:"Course notes 2.1, Example 12", lec:"L07", kind:"example", page:"p. 45",
   q:"Find all values of $x$ satisfying $|x^2-3| \\le 2$.",
   sol:[
    "By property (iv), $-2 \\le x^2-3 \\le 2$, so $1 \\le x^2 \\le 5$.",
    "Take square roots: $\\sqrt1 \\le \\sqrt{x^2} \\le \\sqrt5$, so $1 \\le |x| \\le \\sqrt5$.",
    "Case 1 ($x<0$, $|x|=-x$): $1 \\le -x \\le \\sqrt5$, so $-\\sqrt5 \\le x \\le -1$.",
    "Case 2 ($x\\ge0$, $|x|=x$): $1 \\le x \\le \\sqrt5$.",
    "The solutions are $-\\sqrt5 \\le x \\le -1$ and $1 \\le x \\le \\sqrt5$."],
   ans:"$-\\sqrt5 \\le x \\le -1$ or $1 \\le x \\le \\sqrt5$"},
  {id:"CN2.1.2-XR10", src:"Course notes 2.1, Exercise 10", lec:"L07", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 46",
   q:"Find all values of $x$ satisfying the inequality.<br>(a) $1 < 2x^2-1 < 3$<br>(b) $|2x^3| < 1$<br>(c) $|2x^2-3|>1$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $2 < 2x^2 < 4$, so $1 < x^2 < 2$. Taking square roots, $1 < |x| < \\sqrt2$.",
    "Case 1 ($x<0$, $|x|=-x$): $1<-x<\\sqrt2$, so $-\\sqrt2<x<-1$.",
    "Case 2 ($x\\ge0$, $|x|=x$): $1<x<\\sqrt2$.",
    "So $-\\sqrt2<x<-1$ or $1<x<\\sqrt2$.",
    "(b) By property (iv), $-1 < 2x^3 < 1$, so $-\\frac12 < x^3 < \\frac12$.",
    "Taking cube roots: $\\sqrt[3]{-\\frac12} < x < \\sqrt[3]{\\frac12}$.",
    "(c) By property (v), $2x^2-3<-1$ or $2x^2-3>1$.",
    "First case: $2x^2<2$, so $x^2<1$, $|x|<1$, and by property (iv) $-1<x<1$.",
    "Second case: $2x^2>4$, so $x^2>2$, $|x|>\\sqrt2$, and by property (v) $x<-\\sqrt2$ or $x>\\sqrt2$.",
    "So $x<-\\sqrt2$, or $-1<x<1$, or $x>\\sqrt2$."],
   ans:"(a) $-\\sqrt2<x<-1$ or $1<x<\\sqrt2$; (b) $-\\sqrt[3]{\\frac12} < x < \\sqrt[3]{\\frac12}$; (c) $x<-\\sqrt2$, $-1<x<1$, or $x>\\sqrt2$"},
  {id:"CN2.1.2-EX13", src:"Course notes 2.1, Example 13", lec:"L07", kind:"example", page:"p. 46",
   q:"Determine the derivative of $f(x) = |\\sin(x)|$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\frac{d}{dx}|x| = \\frac{x}{|x|}$, so by the Chain Rule",
    "$f'(x) = \\dfrac{\\sin(x)}{|\\sin(x)|}\\cdot\\cos(x)$"],
   ans:"$f'(x) = \\frac{\\sin(x)}{|\\sin(x)|}\\cos(x)$"},
  {id:"CN2.1.2-EX14", src:"Course notes 2.1, Example 14", lec:"L07", kind:"example", page:"p. 46",
   q:"Determine the derivative of $f(x) = x|x|$.",
   sol:[
    "By the Product Rule,",
    "$f'(x) = 1\\cdot|x| + x\\cdot\\dfrac{x}{|x|} = |x| + \\dfrac{x^2}{\\sqrt{x^2}} = |x| + \\sqrt{x^2} = |x|+|x| = 2|x|$"],
   ans:"$f'(x) = 2|x|$"},
  {id:"CN2.1.2-EX15", src:"Course notes 2.1, Example 15", lec:"L07", kind:"example", page:"p. 47",
   q:"Determine the derivative of $f(x) = \\dfrac{x}{|x|}$.",
   sol:[
    "By the Quotient Rule,",
    "$f'(x) = \\dfrac{1\\cdot|x| - x\\cdot\\frac{x}{|x|}}{|x|^2} = \\dfrac{|x| - \\frac{x^2}{\\sqrt{x^2}}}{|x|^2} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x^2}-\\sqrt{x^2}}{|x|^2} = 0$"],
   ans:"$f'(x) = 0$ (for $x\\ne0$)"},
  {id:"CN2.1.2-XR11", src:"Course notes 2.1, Exercise 11", lec:"L07", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 47",
   q:"Determine the derivative of each function.<br>(a) $f(x) = |x^2+\\cos(x)|$<br>(b) $g(x) = |e^x-1|$",
   sol:[
    "(a) By the Chain Rule, $f'(x) = \\dfrac{x^2+\\cos(x)}{|x^2+\\cos(x)|}\\cdot(2x-\\sin(x))$",
    "(b) By the Chain Rule, $g'(x) = \\dfrac{e^x-1}{|e^x-1|}\\cdot(e^x+0) = \\dfrac{e^x(e^x-1)}{|e^x-1|}$"],
   ans:"(a) $\\frac{x^2+\\cos(x)}{|x^2+\\cos(x)|}(2x-\\sin(x))$; (b) $\\frac{e^x(e^x-1)}{|e^x-1|}$"},
  {id:"CN2.1-P1", src:"Course notes 2.1, End of Section Problem 1", lec:"L06", kind:"problem", page:"p. 47",
   q:"Let $f(x) = \\begin{cases} 2 & \\text{if } -2\\le x<0 \\\\ \\frac1x & \\text{if } 1<x\\le2 \\\\ x-3 & \\text{if } 2<x\\le3\\end{cases}$.<br>(a) Find $f(-1)$, $f(2)$ and $f(2.5)$.<br>(b) Determine the domain of $f$.<br>(c) Sketch the graph of $f$.<br>(d) Use the graph to find the range of $f$.",
   sol:[
    "(a) $-1$ is in $[-2,0)$, so $f(-1)=2$. $2$ is in $(1,2]$, so $f(2)=\\frac12$. $2.5$ is in $(2,3]$, so $f(2.5) = 2.5-3 = -0.5$.",
    "(b) $f$ is defined for $x$ in $[-2,0)$, $(1,2]$ and $(2,3]$, so the domain is $[-2,0)\\cup(1,3]$.",
    "(c) Horizontal segment $y=2$ from a filled dot at $(-2,2)$ to an open dot at $(0,2)$; the curve $y=\\frac1x$ from an open dot at $(1,1)$ to a filled dot at $(2,\\frac12)$; the line $y=x-3$ from an open dot at $(2,-1)$ to a filled dot at $(3,0)$.",
    "(d) The first piece gives only the value 2, the second gives $\\left[\\frac12,1\\right)$ and the third gives $(-1,0]$.",
    "Range: $(-1,0]\\cup\\left[\\frac12,1\\right)\\cup\\{2\\}$"],
   ans:"(a) $2$, $\\frac12$, $-0.5$; (b) $[-2,0)\\cup(1,3]$; (c) see the description; (d) $(-1,0]\\cup\\left[\\frac12,1\\right)\\cup\\{2\\}$"},
  {id:"CN2.1-P2", src:"Course notes 2.1, End of Section Problem 2", lec:"L06", kind:"problem", page:"p. 47",
   q:"The Heaviside Step Function is $H(x) = \\begin{cases}0 & \\text{if } x<0 \\\\ 1 & \\text{if } x>0\\end{cases}$.<br>(a) Find $H(-1)$, $H(2)$ and $H(\\sqrt2)$.<br>(b) Sketch the graph of $H$.<br>(c) Let $f(x) = x^2\\,H(x-2) - (x^2+2x)\\,H(x-4)$. Evaluate $f(1)$, $f(3)$ and $f(5)$.",
   sol:[
    "(a) $H(-1)=0$, $H(2)=1$, $H(\\sqrt2)=1$.",
    "(b) The graph is $y=0$ for $x<0$ and $y=1$ for $x>0$, with open dots at $(0,0)$ and $(0,1)$, since $H(0)$ is not defined.",
    "(c) $f(1) = 1^2H(1-2) - (1^2+2(1))H(1-4) = H(-1) - 3H(-3) = 0-0 = 0$",
    "$f(3) = 3^2H(3-2) - (3^2+2(3))H(3-4) = 9H(1) - 15H(-1) = 9-0 = 9$",
    "$f(5) = 5^2H(5-2) - (5^2+2(5))H(5-4) = 25H(3) - 35H(1) = 25-35 = -10$"],
   ans:"(a) $0$, $1$, $1$; (c) $f(1)=0$, $f(3)=9$, $f(5)=-10$",
   note:"The official solution labels its answers (a) and (b): its (a) covers parts (a) and (b), and its (b) answers part (c)."},
  {id:"CN2.1-P3", src:"Course notes 2.1, End of Section Problem 3", lec:"L06", kind:"problem", page:"p. 47",
   q:"State the domain and sketch the graph of the function.<br>(a) $f(x) = \\begin{cases} 1-x & \\text{if } -2<x<0 \\\\ x^3 & \\text{if } 0\\le x\\le1 \\\\ 2^{-x} & \\text{if } 1<x\\le2\\end{cases}$<br>(b) $f(x) = \\begin{cases} (x+1)^2 & \\text{if } -2\\le x\\le0 \\\\ x+1 & \\text{if } 0<x<2 \\\\ \\sqrt{11-x} & \\text{if } 2<x\\le4\\end{cases}$<br>(c) $f(x) = \\begin{cases} \\frac{1}{x+1} & \\text{if } -2\\le x<-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x^2-1} & \\text{if } -1<x<1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+1} & \\text{if } 1<x\\le2\\end{cases}$",
   sol:[
    "(a) Domain: $(-2,2]$.",
    "Graph: the line $y=1-x$ from an open dot at $(-2,3)$ to an open dot at $(0,1)$; $y=x^3$ from a filled dot at $(0,0)$ to a filled dot at $(1,1)$; $y=2^{-x}$ from an open dot at $(1,\\frac12)$ to a filled dot at $(2,\\frac14)$.",
    "(b) Domain: $[-2,2)\\cup(2,4]$ ($x=2$ is not included in any piece).",
    "Graph: the parabola $y=(x+1)^2$ from a filled dot at $(-2,1)$ through $(-1,0)$ to $(0,1)$; the line $y=x+1$ from $(0,1)$ to an open dot at $(2,3)$; $y=\\sqrt{11-x}$ from an open dot at $(2,3)$ down to a filled dot at $(4,\\sqrt7)$.",
    "(c) Domain: $[-2,-1)\\cup(-1,1)\\cup(1,2]$.",
    "Graph: $y=\\frac1{x+1}$ from a filled dot at $(-2,-1)$ decreasing to $-\\infty$ as $x\\to-1^-$; $y=\\frac1{x^2-1}$ on $(-1,1)$ with maximum $-1$ at $x=0$, going to $-\\infty$ at both ends (vertical asymptotes $x=\\pm1$); $y=\\frac1{x+1}$ from an open dot at $(1,\\frac12)$ to a filled dot at $(2,\\frac13)$."],
   ans:"(a) domain $(-2,2]$; (b) domain $[-2,2)\\cup(2,4]$; (c) domain $[-2,-1)\\cup(-1,1)\\cup(1,2]$"},
  {id:"CN2.1-P4", src:"Course notes 2.1, End of Section Problem 4", lec:"L07", kind:"problem", page:"p. 47",
   q:"Simplify $\\sqrt{(x-1)^2}$ where $x>1$.",
   sol:[
    "By property (i), $\\sqrt{(x-1)^2} = |x-1|$.",
    "Since $x>1$, $x-1>0$, so $|x-1| = x-1$."],
   ans:"$x-1$"},
  {id:"CN2.1-P5", src:"Course notes 2.1, End of Section Problem 5", lec:"L07", kind:"problem", page:"p. 47",
   q:"Simplify $\\sqrt{(x-1)^2}$ where $x<1$.",
   sol:[
    "By property (i), $\\sqrt{(x-1)^2} = |x-1|$.",
    "Since $x<1$, $x-1<0$, so $|x-1| = -(x-1) = -x+1$."],
   ans:"$1-x$"},
  {id:"CN2.1-P6-a-d", src:"Course notes 2.1, End of Section Problem 6, parts (a) to (d)", lec:"L07", kind:"problem", page:"p. 48",
   q:"Find all values of $x$ satisfying the equation or inequality.<br>(a) $|x|=3$<br>(b) $|x|=\\sqrt2$<br>(c) $|x|<\\frac15$<br>(d) $|x|>1$",
   sol:[
    "(a) By property (iii), $x=\\pm3$.",
    "(b) By property (iii), $x=\\pm\\sqrt2$.",
    "(c) By property (iv), $-\\frac15<x<\\frac15$.",
    "(d) By property (v), $x<-1$ or $x>1$."],
   ans:"(a) $x=\\pm3$; (b) $x=\\pm\\sqrt2$; (c) $-\\frac15<x<\\frac15$; (d) $x<-1$ or $x>1$"},
  {id:"CN2.1-P6-e-h", src:"Course notes 2.1, End of Section Problem 6, parts (e) to (h)", lec:"L07", kind:"problem", page:"p. 48",
   q:"Find all values of $x$ satisfying the equation or inequality.<br>(e) $|x|\\le2$<br>(f) $|x|<-2$<br>(g) $|x-2|<0.1$<br>(h) $|3-x|<4$",
   sol:[
    "(e) By property (iv), $-2\\le x\\le2$.",
    "(f) $|x|\\ge0$ for every $x$, so $|x|$ is never less than $-2$. There are no solutions.",
    "(g) By property (iv), $-0.1<x-2<0.1$, so $1.9<x<2.1$.",
    "(h) By property (iv), $-4<3-x<4$, so $-7<-x<1$, so $7>x>-1$.",
    "That is, $-1<x<7$."],
   ans:"(e) $-2\\le x\\le2$; (f) no solutions; (g) $1.9<x<2.1$; (h) $-1<x<7$"},
  {id:"CN2.1-P6-i-l", src:"Course notes 2.1, End of Section Problem 6, parts (i) to (l)", lec:"L07", kind:"problem", page:"p. 48",
   q:"Find all values of $x$ satisfying the equation or inequality.<br>(i) $3|x+3|<1$<br>(j) $|3x+4|<5$<br>(k) $|2x-1|>5$<br>(l) $|2x-1|<3$",
   sol:[
    "(i) $|x+3|<\\frac13$, so by property (iv), $-\\frac13<x+3<\\frac13$.",
    "$-\\frac{10}3<x<-\\frac83$",
    "(j) By property (iv), $-5<3x+4<5$, so $-9<3x<1$, so $-3<x<\\frac13$.",
    "(k) By property (v), $2x-1<-5$ or $2x-1>5$.",
    "So $x<-2$ or $x>3$.",
    "(l) By property (iv), $-3<2x-1<3$, so $-2<2x<4$, so $-1<x<2$."],
   ans:"(i) $-\\frac{10}{3}<x<-\\frac83$; (j) $-3<x<\\frac13$; (k) $x<-2$ or $x>3$; (l) $-1<x<2$"},
  {id:"CN2.1-P6-m-p", src:"Course notes 2.1, End of Section Problem 6, parts (m) to (p)", lec:"L07", kind:"problem", page:"p. 48",
   q:"Find all values of $x$ satisfying the equation or inequality.<br>(m) $2|4x+1|<1$<br>(n) $3|2x-1|<4$<br>(o) $|2-x|\\ge\\frac32$<br>(p) $2|-x-1|\\le\\frac12$",
   sol:[
    "(m) $|4x+1|<\\frac12$, so by property (iv), $-\\frac12<4x+1<\\frac12$.",
    "$-\\frac32<4x<-\\frac12$, so $-\\frac38<x<-\\frac18$.",
    "(n) $|2x-1|<\\frac43$, so by property (iv), $-\\frac43<2x-1<\\frac43$.",
    "$-\\frac13<2x<\\frac73$, so $-\\frac16<x<\\frac76$.",
    "(o) By property (v), $2-x\\le-\\frac32$ or $2-x\\ge\\frac32$.",
    "First: $-x\\le-\\frac72$, so $x\\ge\\frac72$. Second: $-x\\ge-\\frac12$, so $x\\le\\frac12$.",
    "So $x\\le\\frac12$ or $x\\ge\\frac72$.",
    "(p) $|(-1)(x+1)|\\le\\frac14$, and $|(-1)(x+1)| = |-1||x+1| = |x+1|$ by property (ii). So $|x+1|\\le\\frac14$.",
    "By property (iv), $-\\frac14\\le x+1\\le\\frac14$, so $-\\frac54\\le x\\le-\\frac34$."],
   ans:"(m) $-\\frac38<x<-\\frac18$; (n) $-\\frac16<x<\\frac76$; (o) $x\\le\\frac12$ or $x\\ge\\frac72$; (p) $-\\frac54\\le x\\le-\\frac34$"},
  {id:"CN2.1-P6-q-t", src:"Course notes 2.1, End of Section Problem 6, parts (q) to (t)", lec:"L07", kind:"problem", page:"p. 48",
   q:"Find all values of $x$ satisfying the equation or inequality.<br>(q) $2<x^2\\le5$<br>(r) $1\\le x^2-2<3$<br>(s) $7\\le2x^2-1<17$<br>(t) $|x^2-1|<\\frac12$",
   sol:[
    "(q) Take square roots: $\\sqrt2<|x|\\le\\sqrt5$.",
    "Case 1 ($x<0$): $\\sqrt2<-x\\le\\sqrt5$, so $-\\sqrt5\\le x<-\\sqrt2$.",
    "Case 2 ($x\\ge0$): $\\sqrt2<x\\le\\sqrt5$.",
    "(r) $3\\le x^2<5$, so taking square roots, $\\sqrt3\\le|x|<\\sqrt5$.",
    "Case 1 ($x<0$): $\\sqrt3\\le-x<\\sqrt5$, so $-\\sqrt5<x\\le-\\sqrt3$.",
    "Case 2 ($x\\ge0$): $\\sqrt3\\le x<\\sqrt5$.",
    "(s) $8\\le2x^2<18$, so $4\\le x^2<9$, so $2\\le|x|<3$.",
    "Case 1 ($x<0$): $2\\le-x<3$, so $-3<x\\le-2$.",
    "Case 2 ($x\\ge0$): $2\\le x<3$.",
    "(t) By property (iv), $-\\frac12<x^2-1<\\frac12$, so $\\frac12<x^2<\\frac32$, so $\\sqrt{1/2}<|x|<\\sqrt{3/2}$.",
    "Case 1 ($x<0$): $-\\sqrt{3/2}<x<-\\sqrt{1/2}$.",
    "Case 2 ($x\\ge0$): $\\sqrt{1/2}<x<\\sqrt{3/2}$."],
   ans:"(q) $-\\sqrt5\\le x<-\\sqrt2$ or $\\sqrt2<x\\le\\sqrt5$; (r) $-\\sqrt5<x\\le-\\sqrt3$ or $\\sqrt3\\le x<\\sqrt5$; (s) $-3<x\\le-2$ or $2\\le x<3$; (t) $-\\sqrt{3/2}<x<-\\sqrt{1/2}$ or $\\sqrt{1/2}<x<\\sqrt{3/2}$"},
  {id:"CN2.1-P6-u", src:"Course notes 2.1, End of Section Problem 6, part (u)", lec:"L07", kind:"problem", page:"p. 48",
   q:"Find all values of $x$ satisfying the equation or inequality.<br>(u) $|2x^2-3|<1$",
   sol:[
    "(u) By property (iv), $-1<2x^2-3<1$, so $2<2x^2<4$, so $1<x^2<2$, so $1<|x|<\\sqrt2$.",
    "Case 1 ($x<0$): $1<-x<\\sqrt2$, so $-\\sqrt2<x<-1$.",
    "Case 2 ($x\\ge0$): $1<x<\\sqrt2$."],
   ans:"(u) $-\\sqrt2<x<-1$ or $1<x<\\sqrt2$"},
  {id:"CN2.1-P7", src:"Course notes 2.1, End of Section Problem 7", lec:"L07", kind:"problem", page:"p. 48",
   q:"Consider $x\\sqrt{1+\\dfrac1{x^2}}$ where $x<0$.<br>(a) Simplify the expression by factoring $\\frac1{x^2}$ out of the square root (as in Example 7).<br>(b) Simplify the expression by moving the $x$ inside the square root.",
   sol:[
    "(a) $x\\sqrt{1+\\frac1{x^2}} = x\\sqrt{\\frac{x^2}{x^2}+\\frac1{x^2}} = x\\sqrt{\\frac1{x^2}(x^2+1)} = x\\sqrt{\\frac1{x^2}}\\sqrt{x^2+1} = x\\cdot\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2}}\\sqrt{x^2+1} = x\\cdot\\frac1{|x|}\\sqrt{x^2+1}$",
    "Since $x<0$, $|x|=-x$: $x\\cdot\\frac{1}{-x}\\sqrt{x^2+1} = -\\sqrt{x^2+1}$",
    "(b) Since $x<0$, $|x|=-x$, so $x = -|x| = -\\sqrt{x^2}$.",
    "$x\\sqrt{1+\\frac1{x^2}} = -\\sqrt{x^2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac1{x^2}} = -\\sqrt{x^2\\left(1+\\frac1{x^2}\\right)} = -\\sqrt{x^2+1}$"],
   ans:"(a) $-\\sqrt{x^2+1}$; (b) $-\\sqrt{x^2+1}$"},
  {id:"CN2.1-P8", src:"Course notes 2.1, End of Section Problem 8", lec:"L07", kind:"problem", page:"p. 48",
   q:"Simplify $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x^4+x^2}}{x}$ where:<br>(a) $x>0$<br>(b) $x<0$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x^4+x^2}}{x} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x^2(x^2+1)}}{x} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x^2}\\sqrt{x^2+1}}{x} = \\dfrac{|x|\\sqrt{x^2+1}}{x}$",
    "Since $x>0$, $|x| = x$: $\\dfrac{x\\sqrt{x^2+1}}{x} = \\sqrt{x^2+1}$",
    "(b) As in (a), $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x^4+x^2}}{x} = \\dfrac{|x|\\sqrt{x^2+1}}{x}$",
    "Since $x<0$, $|x| = -x$: $\\dfrac{-x\\sqrt{x^2+1}}{x} = -\\sqrt{x^2+1}$"],
   ans:"(a) $\\sqrt{x^2+1}$; (b) $-\\sqrt{x^2+1}$"},
  {id:"CN2.1-P9", src:"Course notes 2.1, End of Section Problem 9", lec:"L07", kind:"problem", page:"p. 48",
   q:"Simplify $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x^4+x^2}}{x^2}$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x^4+x^2}}{x^2} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x^4\\left(1+\\frac1{x^2}\\right)}}{x^2} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x^4}\\sqrt{1+\\frac1{x^2}}}{x^2}$",
    "$\\sqrt{x^4} = |x^2| = x^2$ since $x^2\\ge0$, so this is $\\dfrac{x^2\\sqrt{1+\\frac1{x^2}}}{x^2} = \\sqrt{1+\\dfrac1{x^2}}$"],
   ans:"$\\sqrt{1+\\frac1{x^2}}$"},
  {id:"CN2.1-P10-a-d", src:"Course notes 2.1, End of Section Problem 10, parts (a) to (d)", lec:"L07", kind:"problem", page:"p. 48",
   q:"Determine the derivative of the following functions.<br>(a) $f(x)=|3x+7|$<br>(b) $f(x)=|1-x|$<br>(c) $f(x)=|x^2-1|$<br>(d) $f(x)=|1-\\sqrt x|$",
   sol:[
    "(a) By the Chain Rule, $f'(x) = \\dfrac{3x+7}{|3x+7|}\\cdot3 = \\dfrac{9x+21}{|3x+7|}$",
    "(b) $f'(x) = \\dfrac{1-x}{|1-x|}\\cdot(-1) = \\dfrac{x-1}{|1-x|}$",
    "(c) $f'(x) = \\dfrac{x^2-1}{|x^2-1|}\\cdot(2x) = \\dfrac{2x^3-2x}{|x^2-1|}$",
    "(d) $f'(x) = \\dfrac{1-\\sqrt x}{|1-\\sqrt x|}\\cdot\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt x}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt x-1}{2\\sqrt x\\,|1-\\sqrt x|}$"],
   ans:"(a) $\\frac{9x+21}{|3x+7|}$; (b) $\\frac{x-1}{|1-x|}$; (c) $\\frac{2x^3-2x}{|x^2-1|}$; (d) $\\frac{\\sqrt x-1}{2\\sqrt x|1-\\sqrt x|}$"},
  {id:"CN2.1-P10-e-g", src:"Course notes 2.1, End of Section Problem 10, parts (e) to (g)", lec:"L07", kind:"problem", page:"p. 48",
   q:"Determine the derivative of the following functions.<br>(e) $f(x)=|\\cos(x)|$<br>(f) $f(x) = x\\left|\\dfrac{1}{x^2+1}\\right|$<br>(g) $f(x) = \\dfrac{|x+1|}{x}$",
   sol:[
    "(e) $f'(x) = \\dfrac{\\cos(x)}{|\\cos(x)|}\\cdot(-\\sin(x)) = \\dfrac{-\\sin(x)\\cos(x)}{|\\cos(x)|}$",
    "(f) Since $\\frac1{x^2+1}>0$, $f(x) = \\dfrac{x}{x^2+1}$.",
    "By the Quotient Rule, $f'(x) = \\dfrac{(1)(x^2+1)-x(2x)}{(x^2+1)^2} = \\dfrac{1-x^2}{(x^2+1)^2}$",
    "(g) By the Quotient Rule, $f'(x) = \\dfrac{\\frac{x+1}{|x+1|}\\cdot x - 1\\cdot|x+1|}{x^2}$",
    "Multiply top and bottom by $|x+1|$ and use $|x+1|^2 = (x+1)^2$: $f'(x) = \\dfrac{(x+1)x - (x+1)^2}{x^2|x+1|} = \\dfrac{x^2+x-(x^2+2x+1)}{x^2|x+1|} = \\dfrac{-x-1}{x^2|x+1|}$"],
   ans:"(e) $\\frac{-\\sin(x)\\cos(x)}{|\\cos(x)|}$; (f) $\\frac{1-x^2}{(x^2+1)^2}$; (g) $\\frac{-x-1}{x^2|x+1|}$"},
  {id:"CN2.1-P11", src:"Course notes 2.1, End of Section Problem 11", lec:"L07", kind:"problem", page:"p. 48",
   q:"Evaluate the following integrals. (Use the fact that the integral gives the area under the graph.)<br>(a) $\\displaystyle\\int_0^2|x|\\,dx$<br>(b) $\\displaystyle\\int_1^3|x|\\,dx$<br>(c) $\\displaystyle\\int_{-2}^0|x|\\,dx$<br>(d) $\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^1|x|\\,dx$",
   sol:[
    "(a) The region is a triangle with base 2 and height 2: $\\int_0^2|x|\\,dx = \\frac12(2)(2) = 2$",
    "(b) The region is a triangle with base 2 and height 2 on top of a rectangle with base 2 and height 1:",
    "$\\int_1^3|x|\\,dx = \\frac12(2)(2) + 2(1) = 4$",
    "(c) The region is a triangle with base 2 and height 2: $\\int_{-2}^0|x|\\,dx = 2$",
    "(d) The region is two triangles, each with base 1 and height 1: $\\int_{-1}^1|x|\\,dx = \\frac12+\\frac12 = 1$"],
   ans:"(a) $2$; (b) $4$; (c) $2$; (d) $1$"},
  {id:"CN2.1-P12", src:"Course notes 2.1, End of Section Problem 12", lec:"L07", kind:"problem", page:"p. 48",
   q:"Show that $\\dfrac{x}{|x|} = \\dfrac{|x|}{x}$ for all $x\\ne0$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\dfrac{x}{|x|} = \\dfrac{x}{|x|}\\cdot\\dfrac{|x|}{|x|} = \\dfrac{x|x|}{|x|^2} = \\dfrac{x|x|}{(\\sqrt{x^2})^2} = \\dfrac{x|x|}{x^2} = \\dfrac{|x|}{x}$"],
   ans:"Shown using $|x|^2 = x^2$."},
  {id:"CN2.1-P13", src:"Course notes 2.1, End of Section Problem 13", lec:"L07", kind:"problem", page:"p. 48",
   q:"Explain graphically why the derivative of $|x|$ does not exist at $x=0$.",
   sol:[
    "The graph of $y=|x|$ is a V shape with its vertex at the origin. It has a corner at $x=0$: the slope is $-1$ approaching from the left and $1$ approaching from the right, so there is no single tangent slope there. Hence the derivative does not exist at $x=0$."],
   ans:"The graph has a corner at $x=0$."},
  {id:"CN2.2.1-EX1", src:"Course notes 2.2, Example 1", lec:"L08", kind:"example", page:"p. 49",
   q:"Let $v(t)$ be the velocity of an object in m/s at time $t$ in seconds, so $\\int_a^b v(t)\\,dt$ is the net change in position from $t=a$ to $t=b$. What are the units of this integral, and what does the integral function $d(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_a^x v(t)\\,dt$ represent?",
   sol:[
    "The units of $v(t)\\,dt$ are $\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot\\text{s} = \\text{m}$, so the integral is in meters.",
    "$d(x)$ is the net distance travelled, in meters, from $t=a$ to any time $t=x$."],
   ans:"Meters; $d(x)$ is the net distance travelled from time $a$ to time $x$."},
  {id:"CN2.2.1-EX2", src:"Course notes 2.2, Example 2", lec:"L08", kind:"example", page:"p. 50",
   q:"Let $r(t)$ be the rate in mmol/hr at which urea is produced at time $t$ (hours) in a patient whose kidneys are failing. What does $U(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_0^x r(t)\\,dt$ represent?",
   sol:[
    "The integral of a rate is the net change. Since $r(t)\\ge0$, $U(x)$ is the total amount of urea (in mmol) produced in the $x$ hours after $t=0$."],
   ans:"The total urea (mmol) produced in the first $x$ hours"},
  {id:"CN2.2.1-EX3", src:"Course notes 2.2, Example 3", lec:"L08", kind:"example", page:"p. 50",
   q:"Let $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_1^x t\\,dt$. Evaluate $f(2)$, $f(4)$ and $f(x)$.",
   sol:[
    "An antiderivative of $g(t)=t$ is $G(t) = \\frac12t^2$.",
    "$f(2) = \\int_1^2 t\\,dt = \\tfrac12t^2\\Big|_1^2 = \\tfrac12(2)^2 - \\tfrac12(1)^2 = \\tfrac32$",
    "$f(4) = \\int_1^4 t\\,dt = \\tfrac12t^2\\Big|_1^4 = \\tfrac12(4)^2 - \\tfrac12(1)^2 = \\tfrac{15}2$",
    "$f(x) = \\int_1^x t\\,dt = \\tfrac12t^2\\Big|_1^x = \\tfrac12x^2 - \\tfrac12$"],
   ans:"$f(2)=\\frac32$, $f(4)=\\frac{15}{2}$, $f(x)=\\frac12x^2-\\frac12$"},
  {id:"CN2.2.1-XR1", src:"Course notes 2.2, Exercise 1", lec:"L08", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 50",
   q:"Suppose $\\frac{dL}{dt}$ is the growth rate during month $t$ of an organism, in cm/month. Let $H(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_3^x\\frac{dL}{dt}\\,dt$. What does $H(8)$ represent?",
   sol:[
    "An antiderivative of $\\frac{dL}{dt}$ is $L(t)$, so $H(8) = \\int_3^8\\frac{dL}{dt}\\,dt = L(t)\\Big|_3^8 = L(8)-L(3)$",
    "This is the growth (net change in length) of the organism, in cm, from month 3 to month 8."],
   ans:"The growth in cm from month 3 to month 8"},
  {id:"CN2.2.1-XR2", src:"Course notes 2.2, Exercise 2", lec:"L08", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 50",
   q:"The rate of change of a quantity $Q$ is $Q'(r) = 3r^2$. Write an integral function that represents the net change in $Q$ from $r=1$ to $r=x$. Evaluate the integral function to get an equation for $Q$ in terms of $x$.",
   sol:[
    "The net change from $r=1$ to $r=x$ is $Q(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_1^x 3r^2\\,dr$.",
    "An antiderivative of $3r^2$ is $r^3$, so $Q(x) = r^3\\Big|_1^x = x^3-1$."],
   ans:"$Q(x) = \\int_1^x3r^2\\,dr = x^3-1$",
   note:"The official solution stops at $\\int_1^x 3r^2\\,dr$ and does not evaluate it. The evaluation $x^3-1$ is added here."},
  {id:"CN2.2.1-XR3", src:"Course notes 2.2, Exercise 3", lec:"L08", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 50",
   q:"The growth rate of a population, in thousands, at time $t$ years is $n(t) = e^{-t}$.<br>(a) Let $P(x)$ be the net change in population size between $t=0$ and $t=x$. Write $P(x)$ as an integral function.<br>(b) Evaluate $P(1)$, $P(5)$ and $P(x)$.",
   sol:[
    "(a) Since $n(t)$ is the growth rate, $P(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_0^x n(t)\\,dt = \\int_0^x e^{-t}\\,dt$",
    "(b) An antiderivative of $e^{-t}$ is $-e^{-t}$ (check: $\\frac{d}{dt}(-e^{-t}) = e^{-t}$).",
    "$P(1) = -e^{-t}\\Big|_0^1 = -e^{-1}-(-e^0) = 1-e^{-1}$",
    "$P(5) = -e^{-t}\\Big|_0^5 = -e^{-5}+1 = 1-e^{-5}$",
    "$P(x) = -e^{-t}\\Big|_0^x = 1-e^{-x}$"],
   ans:"(a) $P(x)=\\int_0^xe^{-t}\\,dt$; (b) $P(1)=1-e^{-1}$, $P(5)=1-e^{-5}$, $P(x)=1-e^{-x}$ (thousands)",
   note:"Part (b) uses the reverse chain rule, which the lectures had not covered at this point."},
  {id:"CN2.2.1-EX4", src:"Course notes 2.2, Example 4", lec:"L08", kind:"example", page:"p. 51",
   q:"A function $g$ is given by a graph: a smooth curve with $g(0)=1$, a maximum at $g(1)=2$, then $g(2)=1$ and $g(3)=0$. Let $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_0^x g(t)\\,dt$. Use the left Riemann sum with $n=3$ subdivisions to approximate $f(3)$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\Delta t = \\dfrac{b-a}{n} = \\dfrac{3-0}{3} = 1$ and $t_i = 0 + i(1) = i$ for $0\\le i\\le2$.",
    "Rectangle heights: $g(t_0)=g(0)=1$, $g(t_1)=g(1)=2$, $g(t_2)=g(2)=1$.",
    "$f(3) \\approx g(t_0)\\Delta t + g(t_1)\\Delta t + g(t_2)\\Delta t = 1(1) + 2(1) + 1(1) = 4$"],
   ans:"$f(3)\\approx4$"},
  {id:"CN2.2.1-EX5", src:"Course notes 2.2, Example 5", lec:"L08", kind:"example", page:"pp. 51-52",
   q:"A function $g$ is given by a graph: it starts at $g(0)=1$, drops to about $\\frac12$, stays at $\\frac12$ through $t=\\frac12$, $1$ and $\\frac32$ (so $g(\\frac12)=g(1)=g(\\frac32)=\\frac12$), then falls to $g(2)=0$. Let $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_0^x g(t)\\,dt$. Use the left Riemann sum with $n=4$ subdivisions to approximate $f(2)$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\Delta t = \\dfrac{2-0}{4} = \\dfrac12$ and $t_i = \\dfrac i2$ for $0\\le i\\le3$.",
    "Heights: $g(0)=1$, $g(\\frac12)=\\frac12$, $g(1)=\\frac12$, $g(\\frac32)=\\frac12$.",
    "$f(2) \\approx 1\\cdot\\tfrac12 + \\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac12 + \\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac12 + \\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac12 = \\tfrac54$"],
   ans:"$f(2)\\approx\\frac54$"},
  {id:"CN2.2.1-XR4", src:"Course notes 2.2, Exercise 4", lec:"L08", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 52",
   q:"A function $g$ is given by a graph on $[1,4]$ with $g(1)=1$, $g(2)=2$, $g(3)=3$, dropping to $g(4)=1$. Let $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_1^x g(t)\\,dt$.<br>(a) Use the left Riemann sum with $n=3$ to approximate $f(4)$.<br>(b) Use the left Riemann sum with $n=2$ to approximate $f(3)$.",
   sol:[
    "(a) $\\Delta t = \\dfrac{4-1}{3} = 1$ and $t_i = 1+i$ for $0\\le i\\le2$.",
    "$f(4)\\approx g(1)\\Delta t+g(2)\\Delta t+g(3)\\Delta t = 1(1)+2(1)+3(1) = 6$",
    "(b) $\\Delta t = \\dfrac{3-1}{2} = 1$ and $t_i = 1+i$ for $0\\le i\\le1$.",
    "$f(3)\\approx g(1)\\Delta t + g(2)\\Delta t = 1(1)+2(1) = 3$"],
   ans:"(a) $f(4)\\approx6$; (b) $f(3)\\approx3$"},
  {id:"CN2.2.1-XR5", src:"Course notes 2.2, Exercise 5", lec:"L08", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 52",
   q:"Some values of $g$ are given in the table. Let $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_1^x g(t)\\,dt$. Use the left Riemann sum with $n=4$ to approximate $f(3)$.<table class=\"cn-table\"><tr><th>$t$</th><td>1.0</td><td>1.5</td><td>2.0</td><td>2.5</td></tr><tr><th>$g(t)$</th><td>3</td><td>3.2</td><td>3.4</td><td>3.2</td></tr></table>",
   sol:[
    "$\\Delta t = \\dfrac{3-1}{4} = \\dfrac12$ and $t_i = 1 + \\dfrac i2$ for $0\\le i\\le3$.",
    "$f(3)\\approx g(1)\\Delta t+g(1.5)\\Delta t+g(2)\\Delta t+g(2.5)\\Delta t = 3\\cdot\\tfrac12 + 3.2\\cdot\\tfrac12 + 3.4\\cdot\\tfrac12 + 3.2\\cdot\\tfrac12 = 6.4$"],
   ans:"$f(3)\\approx6.4$"},
  {id:"CN2.2.1-XR6", src:"Course notes 2.2, Exercise 6", lec:"L08", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 52",
   q:"Some values of $g$ are given in the table. Let $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_0^x g(t)\\,dt$. Use the left Riemann sum with $n=5$ to approximate $f(0.5)$.<table class=\"cn-table\"><tr><th>$t$</th><td>0</td><td>0.1</td><td>0.2</td><td>0.3</td><td>0.4</td></tr><tr><th>$g(t)$</th><td>1</td><td>1.01</td><td>1.04</td><td>1.09</td><td>1.17</td></tr></table>",
   sol:[
    "$\\Delta t = \\dfrac{0.5-0}{5} = 0.1$ and $t_i = \\dfrac i{10}$ for $0\\le i\\le4$.",
    "$f(0.5) \\approx 1(0.1) + 1.01(0.1) + 1.04(0.1) + 1.09(0.1) + 1.17(0.1) = 0.531$"],
   ans:"$f(0.5)\\approx0.531$"},
  {id:"CN2.2.1-EX6", src:"Course notes 2.2, Example 6", lec:"L08", kind:"example", page:"p. 53",
   q:"Let $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_1^x t\\sin(t)\\,dt$ and $g(x)=\\cos(x)$. Determine $(f\\circ g)(x)$.",
   sol:[
    "In a composition we replace every $x$ in the outside function by the inside function. In the integral function the only $x$ is the top bound.",
    "$f(g(x)) = \\displaystyle\\int_1^{\\cos(x)} t\\sin(t)\\,dt$"],
   ans:"$\\int_1^{\\cos(x)}t\\sin(t)\\,dt$"},
  {id:"CN2.2.1-EX7", src:"Course notes 2.2, Example 7", lec:"L08", kind:"example", page:"p. 53",
   q:"Let $g(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_{-2}^x e^{t^2}\\,dt$ and $h(x)=x+1$. Determine $(g\\circ h)(x)$.",
   sol:[
    "Replace the top bound $x$ by $h(x) = x+1$:",
    "$g(h(x)) = \\displaystyle\\int_{-2}^{x+1} e^{t^2}\\,dt$"],
   ans:"$\\int_{-2}^{x+1}e^{t^2}\\,dt$"},
  {id:"CN2.2.1-XR7", src:"Course notes 2.2, Exercise 7", lec:"L08", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 53",
   q:"Determine $f(x) = (g\\circ h)(x)$.<br>(a) $g(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_0^x te^t\\,dt$, $h(x) = x^2+x$<br>(b) $g(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_3^x\\frac{1}{t^2+1}\\,dt$, $h(x)=e^x$",
   sol:[
    "(a) Replace the top bound by $h(x)$: $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_0^{x^2+x} te^t\\,dt$",
    "(b) Replace the top bound by $h(x)$: $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_3^{e^x}\\frac1{t^2+1}\\,dt$"],
   ans:"(a) $\\int_0^{x^2+x}te^t\\,dt$; (b) $\\int_3^{e^x}\\frac{1}{t^2+1}\\,dt$"},
  {id:"CN2.2.1-XR8", src:"Course notes 2.2, Exercise 8", lec:"L08", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 53",
   q:"Given the integral function $g(x)$, what function $h(x)$ is needed so that $f(x) = (g\\circ h)(x)$?<br>(a) $g(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_{-2}^x\\sin(t^2)\\,dt$; $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_{-2}^{x^2}\\sin(t^2)\\,dt$<br>(b) $g(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_1^x\\frac{e^t}{t}\\,dt$; $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_1^{x^2+x}\\frac{e^t}{t}\\,dt$",
   sol:[
    "(a) The top bound $x$ has been replaced by $x^2$, so $h(x)=x^2$.",
    "(b) The top bound $x$ has been replaced by $x^2+x$, so $h(x) = x^2+x$."],
   ans:"(a) $h(x)=x^2$; (b) $h(x)=x^2+x$"},
  {id:"CN2.2.2-EX8", src:"Course notes 2.2, Example 8", lec:"L08", kind:"example", page:"p. 54",
   q:"Assume the position of an object is $s(t)$ and its velocity is $v(t)$, so the net distance travelled from $t=a$ to $t=x$ is $\\Delta s(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_a^x v(t)\\,dt$. Explain why $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\displaystyle\\int_a^x v(t)\\,dt = v(x)$.",
   sol:[
    "Take the derivative with respect to $x$ of both sides: $\\Delta s'(x) = \\dfrac{d}{dx}\\displaystyle\\int_a^x v(t)\\,dt$.",
    "The instantaneous rate of change of the net distance travelled at $t=x$ is the object's instantaneous velocity at $t=x$, which is $v(x)$.",
    "So $v(x) = \\dfrac{d}{dx}\\displaystyle\\int_a^x v(t)\\,dt$."],
   ans:"$\\frac{d}{dx}\\int_a^x v(t)\\,dt = v(x)$"},
  {id:"CN2.2.2-EX9", src:"Course notes 2.2, Example 9", lec:"L08", kind:"example", page:"p. 54",
   q:"Let $r(t)$ be the rate at which urea is produced in a patient without functional kidneys.<br>(a) What does $G(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_0^x r(t)\\,dt$ represent?<br>(b) What does $G'(x)$ represent?",
   sol:[
    "(a) $G(x)$ is the total amount of urea in the body $x$ hours after the last dialysis.",
    "(b) $G'(x)$ is the rate of change of the total amount of urea, which is the rate at which urea is produced. So $G'(x) = r(x)$."],
   ans:"(a) total urea $x$ hours after dialysis; (b) $G'(x) = r(x)$, the production rate"},
  {id:"CN2.2.2-EX10", src:"Course notes 2.2, Example 10", lec:"L08", kind:"example", page:"p. 55",
   q:"Evaluate $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\displaystyle\\int_0^x (t^3+t+1)\\,dt$.",
   sol:[
    "By FTC Part 1, $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\displaystyle\\int_0^x(t^3+t+1)\\,dt = x^3+x+1$"],
   ans:"$x^3+x+1$"},
  {id:"CN2.2.2-EX11", src:"Course notes 2.2, Example 11", lec:"L08", kind:"example", page:"p. 55",
   q:"Evaluate $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\displaystyle\\int_3^x (t^3+t+1)\\,dt$.",
   sol:[
    "By FTC Part 1, $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\displaystyle\\int_3^x(t^3+t+1)\\,dt = x^3+x+1$",
    "The lower bound does not change the derivative: the rate of change does not depend on the starting point."],
   ans:"$x^3+x+1$"},
  {id:"CN2.2.2-EX12", src:"Course notes 2.2, Example 12", lec:"L08", kind:"example", page:"p. 55",
   q:"If $f(r) = \\displaystyle\\int_{-3}^r \\frac{\\sin(x)}{x^2+1}\\,dx$, find $f'(r)$.",
   sol:[
    "By FTC Part 1 (the independent variable is $r$), $f'(r) = \\dfrac{\\sin(r)}{r^2+1}$"],
   ans:"$f'(r) = \\frac{\\sin(r)}{r^2+1}$"},
  {id:"CN2.2.2-EX13", src:"Course notes 2.2, Example 13", lec:"L08", kind:"example", page:"p. 55",
   q:"Evaluate $\\dfrac{d}{dw}\\displaystyle\\int_{-2}^w \\sin(\\theta)\\,d\\theta$.",
   sol:[
    "By FTC Part 1, $\\dfrac{d}{dw}\\displaystyle\\int_{-2}^w\\sin(\\theta)\\,d\\theta = \\sin(w)$"],
   ans:"$\\sin(w)$"},
  {id:"CN2.2.2-XR9", src:"Course notes 2.2, Exercise 9", lec:"L08", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 55",
   q:"Determine the derivative of each function.<br>(a) $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_{-2}^x(s^3+s+1)\\,ds$<br>(b) $g(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_1^x t\\sqrt{t^2+1}\\,dt$<br>(c) $h(w) = \\displaystyle\\int_3^w(5x^2+e^{\\cos(x)})\\,dx$",
   sol:[
    "(a) By FTC Part 1, $f'(x) = x^3+x+1$",
    "(b) By FTC Part 1, $g'(x) = x\\sqrt{x^2+1}$",
    "(c) By FTC Part 1, $h'(w) = 5w^2+e^{\\cos(w)}$"],
   ans:"(a) $x^3+x+1$; (b) $x\\sqrt{x^2+1}$; (c) $5w^2+e^{\\cos(w)}$"},
  {id:"CN2.2.2-EX14", src:"Course notes 2.2, Example 14", lec:"L08", kind:"example", page:"p. 56",
   q:"Let $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_2^{x^2}\\left(\\sqrt t + t\\right)dt$. Find $f'(x)$.",
   sol:[
    "Let $g(x) = \\int_2^x(\\sqrt t+t)\\,dt$ and $h(x)=x^2$. Then $f(x) = g(h(x))$.",
    "By FTC Part 1, $g'(x) = \\sqrt x + x$. By the Chain Rule,",
    "$f'(x) = g'(h(x))\\cdot h'(x) = \\left(\\sqrt{x^2}+x^2\\right)(2x)$"],
   ans:"$f'(x) = (\\sqrt{x^2}+x^2)(2x)$"},
  {id:"CN2.2.2-EX15", src:"Course notes 2.2, Example 15", lec:"L08", kind:"example", page:"p. 56",
   q:"Let $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^{e^x}\\cos(t^2)\\,dt$. Find $f'(x)$.",
   sol:[
    "Let $g(x) = \\int_{-1}^x\\cos(t^2)\\,dt$ and $h(x)=e^x$, so $f(x)=g(h(x))$.",
    "By FTC Part 1 and the Chain Rule, $f'(x) = g'(h(x))\\cdot h'(x) = \\cos\\left((e^x)^2\\right)\\cdot e^x = \\cos\\left(e^{2x}\\right)e^x$"],
   ans:"$f'(x) = \\cos(e^{2x})e^x$"},
  {id:"CN2.2.2-EX16", src:"Course notes 2.2, Example 16", lec:"L08", kind:"example", page:"p. 56",
   q:"Let $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^{\\sin(x)} w^2\\,dw$. Find $f'(x)$.",
   sol:[
    "Let $g(x) = \\int_{-1}^x w^2\\,dw$ and $h(x)=\\sin(x)$, so $f(x)=g(h(x))$.",
    "By FTC Part 1 and the Chain Rule, $f'(x) = g'(h(x))\\cdot h'(x) = (\\sin(x))^2\\cos(x)$"],
   ans:"$f'(x) = \\sin^2(x)\\cos(x)$"},
  {id:"CN2.2.2-XR10", src:"Course notes 2.2, Exercise 10", lec:"L08", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 57",
   q:"Find the derivative of the integral function.<br>(a) $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\sqrt x}t^2\\,dt$<br>(b) $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_3^{x^2+x}\\sqrt{2t^2+t}\\,dt$<br>(c) $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_{-2}^{e^{x^2}} t\\sin(t)\\,dt$",
   sol:[
    "(a) By FTC Part 1 and the Chain Rule, $f'(x) = (\\sqrt x)^2\\cdot\\dfrac1{2\\sqrt x} = \\dfrac12\\sqrt x$",
    "(b) By FTC Part 1 and the Chain Rule, $f'(x) = \\sqrt{2(x^2+x)^2+(x^2+x)}\\cdot(2x+1)$",
    "(c) By FTC Part 1 and the Chain Rule, $f'(x) = e^{x^2}\\sin\\left(e^{x^2}\\right)\\cdot2xe^{x^2} = 2xe^{2x^2}\\sin\\left(e^{x^2}\\right)$"],
   ans:"(a) $\\frac12\\sqrt x$; (b) $\\sqrt{2(x^2+x)^2+(x^2+x)}\\,(2x+1)$; (c) $2xe^{2x^2}\\sin(e^{x^2})$"},
  {id:"CN2.2-P1", src:"Course notes 2.2, End of Section Problem 1", lec:"L08", kind:"problem", page:"p. 57",
   q:"Consider $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_0^x 1\\,dt$ for $x\\ge0$.<br>(a) Evaluate $f(0)$, $f(2)$, $f(3)$ and $f(4)$.<br>(b) Sketch the graph of $y=f(x)$.",
   sol:[
    "(a) An antiderivative of $g(t)=1$ is $G(t)=t$.",
    "$f(0) = G(0)-G(0) = 0$, $f(2) = G(2)-G(0) = 2$, $f(3) = 3-0 = 3$, $f(4) = 4-0 = 4$",
    "(b) For any $x\\ge0$, $f(x) = G(x)-G(0) = x$. The graph is the line $y=x$ for $x\\ge0$, starting at the origin."],
   ans:"(a) $0, 2, 3, 4$; (b) $f(x)=x$, the line $y=x$ for $x\\ge0$"},
  {id:"CN2.2-P2", src:"Course notes 2.2, End of Section Problem 2", lec:"L08", kind:"problem", page:"p. 57",
   q:"Consider $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_3^x 1\\,dt$ for $x\\ge0$.<br>(a) Evaluate $f(0)$, $f(2)$, $f(3)$ and $f(4)$.<br>(b) Sketch the graph of $y=f(x)$.",
   sol:[
    "(a) An antiderivative of $g(t)=1$ is $G(t)=t$.",
    "$f(0) = G(0)-G(3) = 0-3 = -3$, $f(2) = 2-3 = -1$, $f(3) = 3-3 = 0$, $f(4) = 4-3 = 1$",
    "(b) For any $x\\ge0$, $f(x) = G(x)-G(3) = x-3$. The graph is the line $y=x-3$ for $x\\ge0$, from $(0,-3)$ through $(3,0)$."],
   ans:"(a) $-3, -1, 0, 1$; (b) $f(x)=x-3$ for $x\\ge0$"},
  {id:"CN2.2-P3", src:"Course notes 2.2, End of Section Problem 3", lec:"L08", kind:"problem", page:"p. 57",
   q:"Consider $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_{-2}^x t\\,dt$ for $x\\ge-2$.<br>(a) Evaluate $f(-2)$, $f(-1)$, $f(0)$ and $f(1)$.<br>(b) Sketch the graph of $y=f(x)$.",
   sol:[
    "(a) An antiderivative of $g(t)=t$ is $G(t) = \\frac12t^2$, and $G(-2) = 2$.",
    "$f(-2) = G(-2)-G(-2) = 0$",
    "$f(-1) = \\frac{(-1)^2}2 - 2 = -\\frac32$, $f(0) = 0-2 = -2$, $f(1) = \\frac{1}2-2 = -\\frac32$",
    "(b) For any $x\\ge-2$, $f(x) = \\frac12x^2-\\frac12(-2)^2 = \\frac12x^2-2$. The graph is the parabola $y=\\frac12x^2-2$ for $x\\ge-2$: it starts at $(-2,0)$, has minimum $(0,-2)$ and crosses the $x$-axis again at $x=2$."],
   ans:"(a) $0, -\\frac32, -2, -\\frac32$; (b) $f(x)=\\frac12x^2-2$ for $x\\ge-2$"},
  {id:"CN2.2-P4", src:"Course notes 2.2, End of Section Problem 4", lec:"L08", kind:"problem", page:"p. 57",
   q:"Determine $f(x) = (g\\circ h)(x)$.<br>(a) $g(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_1^x\\sin^3(t)\\,dt$, $h(x)=2x+1$<br>(b) $g(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_0^x e^{-t^2}\\,dt$, $h(x)=-x$<br>(c) $g(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_2^x\\frac{\\sin(t)}{t}\\,dt$, $h(x)=x^2$<br>(d) $g(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^x|t+\\cos(t)|\\,dt$, $h(x)=e^x$<br>(e) $g(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_0^x\\sqrt{t^2+1}\\,dt$, $h(x)=\\sin(x)$",
   sol:[
    "(a) Replace the top bound by $h(x)$: $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_1^{2x+1}\\sin^3(t)\\,dt$",
    "(b) $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_0^{-x}e^{-t^2}\\,dt$",
    "(c) $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_2^{x^2}\\frac{\\sin(t)}{t}\\,dt$",
    "(d) $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^{e^x}|t+\\cos(t)|\\,dt$",
    "(e) $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\sin(x)}\\sqrt{t^2+1}\\,dt$"],
   ans:"(a) $\\int_1^{2x+1}\\sin^3(t)\\,dt$; (b) $\\int_0^{-x}e^{-t^2}\\,dt$; (c) $\\int_2^{x^2}\\frac{\\sin(t)}{t}\\,dt$; (d) $\\int_{-1}^{e^x}|t+\\cos(t)|\\,dt$; (e) $\\int_0^{\\sin(x)}\\sqrt{t^2+1}\\,dt$"},
  {id:"CN2.2-P5-a-d", src:"Course notes 2.2, End of Section Problem 5, parts (a) to (d)", lec:"L08", kind:"problem", page:"p. 57",
   q:"Determine the derivative of the following functions.<br>(a) $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_0^x(t^2+\\sqrt t)\\,dt$<br>(b) $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_{-7}^x\\ln(t^2+1)\\,dt$<br>(c) $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_{17}^x(w^3-w)\\,dw$<br>(d) $g(w) = \\displaystyle\\int_3^w te^t\\,dt$",
   sol:[
    "(a) By FTC Part 1, $f'(x) = x^2+\\sqrt x$",
    "(b) By FTC Part 1, $f'(x) = \\ln(x^2+1)$",
    "(c) By FTC Part 1, $f'(x) = x^3-x$",
    "(d) By FTC Part 1, $g'(w) = we^w$"],
   ans:"(a) $x^2+\\sqrt x$; (b) $\\ln(x^2+1)$; (c) $x^3-x$; (d) $we^w$"},
  {id:"CN2.2-P5-e-h", src:"Course notes 2.2, End of Section Problem 5, parts (e) to (h)", lec:"L08", kind:"problem", page:"p. 57",
   q:"Determine the derivative of the following functions.<br>(e) $h(r) = \\displaystyle\\int_{-3}^r 1\\,dx$<br>(f) $j(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_\\pi^x\\tan(t^2+t)\\,dt$<br>(g) $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_0^x(r^2+r)\\,dr$<br>(h) $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_{-5}^{3x}(s^2+s)\\,ds$",
   sol:[
    "(e) By FTC Part 1, $h'(r) = 1$",
    "(f) By FTC Part 1, $j'(x) = \\tan(x^2+x)$",
    "(g) By FTC Part 1, $f'(x) = x^2+x$",
    "(h) By FTC Part 1 and the Chain Rule, $f'(x) = ((3x)^2+3x)\\cdot3 = 27x^2+9x$"],
   ans:"(e) $1$; (f) $\\tan(x^2+x)$; (g) $x^2+x$; (h) $27x^2+9x$"},
  {id:"CN2.2-P5-i-l", src:"Course notes 2.2, End of Section Problem 5, parts (i) to (l)", lec:"L08", kind:"problem", page:"p. 57",
   q:"Determine the derivative of the following functions.<br>(i) $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\sin(x)}\\frac1r\\,dr$<br>(j) $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_3^{2x}(w^2+w)^{1/3}\\,dw$<br>(k) $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_0^{x^2+\\frac1x}e^t\\,dt$<br>(l) $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_4^{3x+e^x}\\sqrt{w+1}\\,dw$",
   sol:[
    "(i) By FTC Part 1 and the Chain Rule, $f'(x) = \\dfrac1{\\sin(x)}\\cdot\\cos(x) = \\cot(x)$",
    "(j) By FTC Part 1 and the Chain Rule, $f'(x) = ((2x)^2+2x)^{1/3}\\cdot2 = 2(4x^2+2x)^{1/3}$",
    "(k) By FTC Part 1 and the Chain Rule, $f'(x) = e^{x^2+\\frac1x}\\cdot\\left(2x-\\dfrac1{x^2}\\right)$",
    "(l) By FTC Part 1 and the Chain Rule, $f'(x) = \\sqrt{3x+e^x+1}\\cdot(3+e^x)$"],
   ans:"(i) $\\cot(x)$; (j) $2(4x^2+2x)^{1/3}$; (k) $e^{x^2+\\frac1x}\\left(2x-\\frac1{x^2}\\right)$; (l) $\\sqrt{3x+e^x+1}\\,(3+e^x)$"},
  {id:"CN2.2-P5-m", src:"Course notes 2.2, End of Section Problem 5, part (m)", lec:"L08", kind:"problem", page:"p. 57",
   q:"Determine the derivative of the following functions.<br>(m) $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\cos(x)+x}\\frac{1}{1+t^2}\\,dt$",
   sol:[
    "(m) By FTC Part 1 and the Chain Rule, $f'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{1+(\\cos(x)+x)^2}\\cdot(1-\\sin(x))$"],
   ans:"(m) $\\frac{1-\\sin(x)}{1+(\\cos(x)+x)^2}$"},
  {id:"CN2.2-P6", src:"Course notes 2.2, End of Section Problem 6", lec:"L08", kind:"problem", page:"p. 58",
   q:"Let $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_0^x g(t)\\,dt$. Use the table to approximate $f(2)$ using 4 subdivisions.<table class=\"cn-table\"><tr><th>$t$</th><td>0</td><td>0.5</td><td>1</td><td>1.5</td></tr><tr><th>$g(t)$</th><td>1.0</td><td>1.2</td><td>0.8</td><td>1.4</td></tr></table>",
   sol:[
    "$\\Delta t = \\dfrac{2-0}{4} = \\dfrac12$ and $t_i = \\dfrac i2$ for $0\\le i\\le3$.",
    "$f(2)\\approx g(0)\\Delta t+g(0.5)\\Delta t+g(1)\\Delta t+g(1.5)\\Delta t = 1.0\\cdot\\tfrac12+1.2\\cdot\\tfrac12+0.8\\cdot\\tfrac12+1.4\\cdot\\tfrac12 = \\tfrac{11}5$"],
   ans:"$f(2)\\approx\\frac{11}{5}$"},
  {id:"CN2.2-P7", src:"Course notes 2.2, End of Section Problem 7", lec:"L08", kind:"problem", page:"p. 58",
   q:"Let $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_0^x g(t)\\,dt$. Use the table to approximate $f(4)$ using 4 subdivisions.<table class=\"cn-table\"><tr><th>$t$</th><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><th>$g(t)$</th><td>0.40</td><td>0.35</td><td>0.33</td><td>0.32</td></tr></table>",
   sol:[
    "$\\Delta t = \\dfrac{4-0}4 = 1$ and $t_i = i$ for $0\\le i\\le3$.",
    "$f(4)\\approx 0.40(1)+0.35(1)+0.33(1)+0.32(1) = 1.40$"],
   ans:"$f(4)\\approx1.40$"},
  {id:"CN2.2-P8", src:"Course notes 2.2, End of Section Problem 8", lec:"L08", kind:"problem", page:"p. 58",
   q:"Let $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_1^x g(t)\\,dt$. Use the table to approximate $f(2)$ using 3 subdivisions.<table class=\"cn-table\"><tr><th>$t$</th><td>1</td><td>$\\frac43$</td><td>$\\frac53$</td></tr><tr><th>$g(t)$</th><td>0.2</td><td>$-0.1$</td><td>$-0.3$</td></tr></table>",
   sol:[
    "$\\Delta t = \\dfrac{2-1}3 = \\dfrac13$ and $t_i = 1+\\dfrac i3$ for $0\\le i\\le2$.",
    "$f(2)\\approx 0.2\\cdot\\tfrac13 + (-0.1)\\cdot\\tfrac13 + (-0.3)\\cdot\\tfrac13 = -\\tfrac{0.2}{3} = -\\tfrac1{15}$"],
   ans:"$f(2)\\approx-\\frac1{15}$"},
  {id:"CN2.2-P9", src:"Course notes 2.2, End of Section Problem 9", lec:"L08", kind:"problem", page:"p. 58",
   q:"Let $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^x g(t)\\,dt$. Use the table to approximate $f(1)$ using 4 subdivisions.<table class=\"cn-table\"><tr><th>$t$</th><td>$-1.0$</td><td>$-0.5$</td><td>0</td><td>0.5</td></tr><tr><th>$g(t)$</th><td>12</td><td>23</td><td>32</td><td>42</td></tr></table>",
   sol:[
    "$\\Delta t = \\dfrac{1-(-1)}4 = \\dfrac12$ and $t_i = -1+\\dfrac i2$ for $0\\le i\\le3$.",
    "$f(1)\\approx 12\\cdot\\tfrac12+23\\cdot\\tfrac12+32\\cdot\\tfrac12+42\\cdot\\tfrac12 = \\tfrac{109}2$"],
   ans:"$f(1)\\approx\\frac{109}{2}$"},
  {id:"CN2.2-P10", src:"Course notes 2.2, End of Section Problem 10", lec:"L08", kind:"problem", page:"p. 58",
   q:"Let $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_3^x g(t)\\,dt$. Use the table to approximate $f(4)$ using 4 subdivisions.<table class=\"cn-table\"><tr><th>$t$</th><td>3.0</td><td>3.25</td><td>3.5</td><td>3.75</td></tr><tr><th>$g(t)$</th><td>$-2.2$</td><td>$-2.0$</td><td>$-1.6$</td><td>$-1.0$</td></tr></table>",
   sol:[
    "$\\Delta t = \\dfrac{4-3}4 = \\dfrac14$ and $t_i = 3+\\dfrac i4$ for $0\\le i\\le3$.",
    "$f(4)\\approx (-2.2)\\cdot\\tfrac14+(-2.0)\\cdot\\tfrac14+(-1.6)\\cdot\\tfrac14+(-1.0)\\cdot\\tfrac14 = -\\tfrac{6.8}{4} = -1.7$"],
   ans:"$f(4)\\approx-1.7$"},
  {id:"CN2.2-P11", src:"Course notes 2.2, End of Section Problem 11", lec:"L08", kind:"problem", page:"p. 58",
   q:"Let $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_1^x g(t)\\,dt$. Use the table to approximate $f(2)$ using 5 subdivisions.<table class=\"cn-table\"><tr><th>$t$</th><td>1.0</td><td>1.2</td><td>1.4</td><td>1.6</td><td>1.8</td></tr><tr><th>$g(t)$</th><td>0.2</td><td>0.4</td><td>0.4</td><td>0.4</td><td>0.6</td></tr></table>",
   sol:[
    "$\\Delta t = \\dfrac{2-1}5 = \\dfrac15$ and $t_i = 1+\\dfrac i5$ for $0\\le i\\le4$.",
    "$f(2)\\approx 0.2\\cdot\\tfrac15+0.4\\cdot\\tfrac15+0.4\\cdot\\tfrac15+0.4\\cdot\\tfrac15+0.6\\cdot\\tfrac15 = \\tfrac{2}{5}$"],
   ans:"$f(2)\\approx\\frac25$"},
  {id:"CN2.2-P12", src:"Course notes 2.2, End of Section Problem 12", lec:"L08", kind:"problem", page:"p. 58",
   q:"The size of a mosquito population is changing at a rate of $432t^2-5t^4$ per month, where $t$ is in months. What does $f(t) = \\displaystyle\\int_0^t(432x^2-5x^4)\\,dx$ represent?",
   sol:[
    "The integral of a rate is the net change in the quantity. So $f(t)$ is the net change in the size of the mosquito population from month 0 to month $t$."],
   ans:"The net change in the mosquito population from month 0 to month $t$"},
  {id:"CN2.2-P13", src:"Course notes 2.2, End of Section Problem 13", lec:"L08", kind:"problem", page:"p. 58",
   q:"The end product of a chemical reaction is produced at a rate of $\\dfrac{\\sqrt t-1}{t}$ mg/min. What does $f(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_0^x\\frac{\\sqrt t-1}{t}\\,dt$ represent?",
   sol:[
    "The integral of a rate is the net change in the quantity. So $f(x)$ is the net amount of product, in mg, produced from the lower limit of the integral up to time $t=x$ minutes."],
   ans:"The net amount of product (mg) made up to time $x$ minutes",
   note:"The problem prints the lower limit as 0, but the official solution says \"from time $t=1$ to time $t=x$\". The rate $\\frac{\\sqrt t-1}{t}$ is not defined at $t=0$, so a lower limit of 1 is probably intended."},
  {id:"CN2.2-P14", src:"Course notes 2.2, End of Section Problem 14", lec:"L08", kind:"problem", page:"p. 58",
   q:"An object has velocity $v(t)=t+1$ m/s. Determine the net change in the object's position from time $t=2$ to:<br>(a) $t=3$<br>(b) $t=4$<br>(c) $t=x$",
   sol:[
    "The net change in position from $t=2$ to $t=x$ is $\\Delta s = \\int_2^x v(t)\\,dt$. An antiderivative of $v(t)=t+1$ is $s(t) = \\frac12t^2+t$.",
    "(a) $\\int_2^3(t+1)\\,dt = \\left(\\frac{3^2}2+3\\right)-\\left(\\frac{2^2}2+2\\right) = \\frac{15}2 - 4 = \\frac72$ m",
    "(b) $\\int_2^4(t+1)\\,dt = \\left(\\frac{4^2}2+4\\right)-\\left(\\frac{2^2}2+2\\right) = 12-4 = 8$ m",
    "(c) $\\int_2^x(t+1)\\,dt = \\left(\\frac{x^2}2+x\\right)-\\left(\\frac{2^2}2+2\\right) = \\frac{x^2}2+x-4$ m, for any $x\\ge2$"],
   ans:"(a) $\\frac72$ m; (b) $8$ m; (c) $\\frac{x^2}{2}+x-4$ m"},
  {id:"CN2.2-P15", src:"Course notes 2.2, End of Section Problem 15", lec:"L08", kind:"problem", page:"p. 58",
   q:"The rate of change of the mass of an object is $f(t)=\\sin(t)$ kg/h. Determine the net change in the object's mass from $t=0$ to:<br>(a) $t=\\frac\\pi4$<br>(b) $t=\\pi$<br>(c) $t=x$",
   sol:[
    "The net change is $\\Delta m = \\int_0^x f(t)\\,dt$. An antiderivative of $\\sin(t)$ is $F(t) = -\\cos(t)$.",
    "(a) $\\int_0^{\\pi/4}\\sin(t)\\,dt = -\\cos(t)\\Big|_0^{\\pi/4} = -\\cos\\left(\\frac\\pi4\\right)+\\cos(0) = 1-\\frac{\\sqrt2}2$ kg",
    "(b) $\\int_0^\\pi\\sin(t)\\,dt = -\\cos(\\pi)+\\cos(0) = 2$ kg",
    "(c) $\\int_0^x\\sin(t)\\,dt = -\\cos(x)+\\cos(0) = 1-\\cos(x)$ kg, for any $x\\ge0$"],
   ans:"(a) $1-\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}$ kg; (b) $2$ kg; (c) $1-\\cos(x)$ kg"},
  {id:"CN2.2-P16", src:"Course notes 2.2, End of Section Problem 16", lec:"L08", kind:"problem", page:"p. 58",
   q:"The rate of change of air pressure at altitude $h$ is $-3e^{-0.21h}$ kPa/km. Determine the net change in air pressure from $h=0$ to:<br>(a) $h=1$<br>(b) $h=2$<br>(c) $h=x$",
   sol:[
    "The net change is $\\Delta P = \\int_0^x -3e^{-0.21h}\\,dh$.",
    "An antiderivative of $-3e^{-0.21h}$ is $F(h) = \\frac{-3}{-0.21}e^{-0.21h} = \\frac{100}7e^{-0.21h}$ (check: $F'(h) = \\frac{100}{7}(-0.21)e^{-0.21h} = -3e^{-0.21h}$).",
    "(a) $\\int_0^1 -3e^{-0.21h}\\,dh = \\frac{100}7e^{-0.21h}\\Big|_0^1 = \\frac{100}7e^{-0.21}-\\frac{100}7$ kPa",
    "(b) $\\int_0^2 -3e^{-0.21h}\\,dh = \\frac{100}7e^{-0.42}-\\frac{100}7$ kPa",
    "(c) $\\int_0^x -3e^{-0.21h}\\,dh = \\frac{100}7e^{-0.21x}-\\frac{100}7$ kPa, for any $x\\ge0$"],
   ans:"(a) $\\frac{100}{7}e^{-0.21}-\\frac{100}{7}$ kPa; (b) $\\frac{100}{7}e^{-0.42}-\\frac{100}{7}$ kPa; (c) $\\frac{100}{7}e^{-0.21x}-\\frac{100}{7}$ kPa",
   note:"Uses the reverse chain rule, which the lectures had not covered at this point."},
  {id:"CN2.3.1-EX1", src:"Course notes 2.3, Example 1", lec:"L09", kind:"example", page:"p. 59",
   q:"Find the inverse, $f^{-1}(y)$, of $f(x) = 3x+5$.",
   sol:[
    "Set $y=3x+5$ and solve for $x$: $y-5 = 3x$, so $x = \\dfrac{y-5}3$.",
    "$f^{-1}(y) = \\dfrac{y-5}3$"],
   ans:"$f^{-1}(y) = \\frac{y-5}{3}$"},
  {id:"CN2.3.1-EX2", src:"Course notes 2.3, Example 2", lec:"L09", kind:"example", page:"p. 60",
   q:"Find the inverse, $g^{-1}(y)$, of $g(x) = x^2$ on $[0,\\infty)$ and on $(-\\infty,0)$.",
   sol:[
    "Set $y=x^2$ and solve for $x$: $\\sqrt y = \\sqrt{x^2} = |x|$.",
    "On $[0,\\infty)$: $x\\ge0$, so $|x|=x$ and $\\sqrt y = x$. Thus $g^{-1}(y) = \\sqrt y$.",
    "On $(-\\infty,0)$: $x<0$, so $|x|=-x$ and $\\sqrt y = -x$, so $x = -\\sqrt y$. Thus $g^{-1}(y) = -\\sqrt y$."],
   ans:"$\\sqrt y$ on $[0,\\infty)$; $-\\sqrt y$ on $(-\\infty,0)$"},
  {id:"CN2.3.1-EX3", src:"Course notes 2.3, Example 3", lec:"L09", kind:"example", page:"p. 60",
   q:"Let $p(t) = t^3+3$ be the population of bacteria in a culture (in millions) at time $t\\ge0$ hours. Find $p^{-1}$ and use it to find the time when the population reaches 100 million.",
   sol:[
    "Set $p(t)=P$ and solve for $t$: $P = t^3+3$, so $P-3 = t^3$, so $t = \\sqrt[3]{P-3}$.",
    "$p^{-1}(P) = \\sqrt[3]{P-3}$. This function takes a population and outputs a time.",
    "The population reaches 100 million at $t = p^{-1}(100) = \\sqrt[3]{100-3} = \\sqrt[3]{97}$ hours."],
   ans:"$p^{-1}(P) = \\sqrt[3]{P-3}$; $t = \\sqrt[3]{97}$ h"},
  {id:"CN2.3.1-XR1", src:"Course notes 2.3, Exercise 1", lec:"L09", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 61",
   q:"If $H(a) = \\dfrac{100a}{100+a}$ is the height of a tree in meters at age $a$ years, find $H^{-1}$ and use it to find the age of a tree that is 50 meters high.",
   sol:[
    "Let $H(a) = h$: $h = \\dfrac{100a}{100+a}$",
    "$h(100+a) = 100a \\Rightarrow 100h + ha = 100a \\Rightarrow 100h = a(100-h) \\Rightarrow a = \\dfrac{100h}{100-h}$",
    "So $H^{-1}(h) = \\dfrac{100h}{100-h}$.",
    "The age of a 50 m tree is $a = H^{-1}(50) = \\dfrac{100(50)}{100-50} = 100$ years."],
   ans:"$H^{-1}(h) = \\frac{100h}{100-h}$; 100 years"},
  {id:"CN2.3.1-EX4", src:"Course notes 2.3, Example 4", lec:"L09", kind:"example", page:"p. 61",
   q:"Show that $f(x) = x^2$ is not invertible on $[-2,2]$.",
   sol:[
    "The horizontal line $y=2$ meets the graph of $y=x^2$ at two points in $[-2,2]$, namely $x=-\\sqrt2$ and $x=\\sqrt2$.",
    "By the Horizontal Line Test, $f(x)=x^2$ is not invertible on $[-2,2]$."],
   ans:"Fails the Horizontal Line Test ($f(-\\sqrt2)=f(\\sqrt2)=2$)"},
  {id:"CN2.3.1-XR2", src:"Course notes 2.3, Exercise 2", lec:"L09", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 61",
   q:"Show that $f(x) = x^2-2x+1$ is not invertible on $[0,2]$.",
   sol:[
    "$f(0)=1$ and $f(2)=4-4+1=1$, so the horizontal line $y=1$ meets the graph at two points in $[0,2]$.",
    "By the Horizontal Line Test, $f$ is not invertible on $[0,2]$."],
   ans:"$f(0)=f(2)=1$, so it fails the Horizontal Line Test"},
  {id:"CN2.3.1-XR3", src:"Course notes 2.3, Exercise 3", lec:"L09", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 61",
   q:"Explain why $\\sin(x)$ is invertible on $\\left[-\\frac\\pi2,\\frac\\pi2\\right]$ but not on $[0,\\pi]$. Give another interval on which $\\sin(x)$ is invertible.",
   sol:[
    "On $\\left[-\\frac\\pi2,\\frac\\pi2\\right]$ the graph of $\\sin$ is increasing from $-1$ to $1$, so every horizontal line meets it at most once. By the Horizontal Line Test it is invertible there.",
    "On $[0,\\pi]$ the graph rises from 0 to 1 and falls back to 0, so for example $\\sin(\\frac\\pi6) = \\sin(\\frac{5\\pi}6) = \\frac12$. It fails the Horizontal Line Test.",
    "Another interval where $\\sin$ is invertible is $\\left[\\frac\\pi2,\\frac{3\\pi}2\\right]$."],
   ans:"Increasing on $[-\\frac\\pi2,\\frac\\pi2]$; rises then falls on $[0,\\pi]$; e.g. $[\\frac\\pi2,\\frac{3\\pi}2]$"},
  {id:"CN2.3.1-XR4", src:"Course notes 2.3, Exercise 4", lec:"L09", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 61",
   q:"Give an example of a function $f$ that is defined for all real numbers but does not have an inverse on any interval $(a,b)$.",
   sol:[
    "The constant function $y=1$: on any interval $(a,b)$ the horizontal line $y=1$ meets the graph infinitely many times, so it fails the Horizontal Line Test on every interval."],
   ans:"$f(x) = 1$ (any constant function)"},
  {id:"CN2.3.2-EX5", src:"Course notes 2.3, Example 5", lec:"L09", kind:"example", page:"p. 62",
   q:"Given that $f(x) = x^3+2x+1$ is invertible, find $f^{-1}(1)$ and $f^{-1}(4)$.",
   sol:[
    "$f^{-1}(1)$ asks: what $x$ gives $f(x)=1$? By inspection $f(0)=1$, so $f^{-1}(1)=0$.",
    "Similarly $f(1) = 1+2+1 = 4$, so $f^{-1}(4)=1$."],
   ans:"$f^{-1}(1)=0$, $f^{-1}(4)=1$"},
  {id:"CN2.3.2-EX6", src:"Course notes 2.3, Example 6", lec:"L09", kind:"example", page:"p. 62",
   q:"Given that $f(x) = \\sin(x)$ is invertible on $\\left[-\\frac\\pi2,\\frac\\pi2\\right]$, evaluate $f^{-1}\\left(\\frac12\\right)$.",
   sol:[
    "We need the angle $\\theta$ with $-\\frac\\pi2\\le\\theta\\le\\frac\\pi2$ and $\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac12$.",
    "$\\sin\\left(\\frac\\pi6\\right) = \\frac12$ and $\\frac\\pi6$ is in the interval, so $f^{-1}\\left(\\frac12\\right) = \\frac\\pi6$."],
   ans:"$\\frac\\pi6$"},
  {id:"CN2.3.2-XR5", src:"Course notes 2.3, Exercise 5", lec:"L09", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 62",
   q:"Given that $f(x) = x^3+x^2+x+1$ is invertible, find $f^{-1}(1)$ and $f^{-1}(0)$.",
   sol:[
    "$f(0) = 0+0+0+1 = 1$, so $f^{-1}(1)=0$.",
    "$f(-1) = -1+1-1+1 = 0$, so $f^{-1}(0) = -1$."],
   ans:"$f^{-1}(1)=0$, $f^{-1}(0)=-1$"},
  {id:"CN2.3.2-XR6", src:"Course notes 2.3, Exercise 6", lec:"L09", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 62",
   q:"Given that $f(x)=\\cos(x)$ is invertible on $[0,\\pi]$, evaluate $f^{-1}\\left(\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\right)$.",
   sol:[
    "We need $x$ in $[0,\\pi]$ with $\\cos(x) = \\frac1{\\sqrt2}$. Since $\\cos\\left(\\frac\\pi4\\right) = \\frac1{\\sqrt2}$, $f^{-1}\\left(\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\right) = \\frac\\pi4$."],
   ans:"$\\frac\\pi4$"},
  {id:"CN2.3.2-XR7", src:"Course notes 2.3, Exercise 7", lec:"L09", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 62",
   q:"Given that $f(x) = x^2+2x$ is invertible on $(-\\infty,-1)$, find $f^{-1}(0)$.",
   sol:[
    "We need $x<-1$ with $f(x)=0$. $f(-2) = 4-4 = 0$ and $-2<-1$, so $f^{-1}(0) = -2$."],
   ans:"$-2$"},
  {id:"CN2.3.2-EX7", src:"Course notes 2.3, Example 7", lec:"L09", kind:"example", page:"p. 63",
   q:"Let $f(x) = \\sqrt{x+2}$. What is the domain and range of $f^{-1}$?",
   sol:[
    "$f$ is invertible on its domain, so take $I = [-2,\\infty)$.",
    "The domain of $f^{-1}$ is the set of values $f(x)$ for $x$ in $I$, which is $[0,\\infty)$.",
    "The range of $f^{-1}$ is $I = [-2,\\infty)$."],
   ans:"Domain $[0,\\infty)$, range $[-2,\\infty)$"},
  {id:"CN2.3.2-EX8", src:"Course notes 2.3, Example 8", lec:"L09", kind:"example", page:"p. 63",
   q:"Let $f(x) = x^2$, with an inverse $f^{-1}$ defined on $I = (-\\infty,0)$. What is the domain and range of $f^{-1}$?",
   sol:[
    "The domain of $f^{-1}$ is the set of values $f(x)$ for $x$ in $I$: $(0,\\infty)$.",
    "The range of $f^{-1}$ is $I = (-\\infty,0)$."],
   ans:"Domain $(0,\\infty)$, range $(-\\infty,0)$"},
  {id:"CN2.3.2-XR8", src:"Course notes 2.3, Exercise 8", lec:"L09", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 63",
   q:"Given that $f(x) = x^2+2x$ is invertible on $I = [-1,\\infty)$, find the domain and range of $f^{-1}$.",
   sol:[
    "$f(x) = (x+1)^2-1$ takes every value in $[-1,\\infty)$ for $-1\\le x<\\infty$, so the domain of $f^{-1}$ is $[-1,\\infty)$.",
    "The range of $f^{-1}$ is $I = [-1,\\infty)$."],
   ans:"Domain $[-1,\\infty)$, range $[-1,\\infty)$"},
  {id:"CN2.3.2-EX9", src:"Course notes 2.3, Example 9", lec:"L09", kind:"example", page:"p. 63",
   q:"$f(x) = \\cos(x)$ is invertible on $I = [0,\\pi]$. We denote this inverse by arccos. What is the domain and range of arccos?",
   sol:[
    "$\\cos(x)$ takes every value from $-1$ to $1$ for $0\\le x\\le\\pi$, so the domain of arccos is $[-1,1]$.",
    "The range of arccos is $I = [0,\\pi]$."],
   ans:"Domain $[-1,1]$, range $[0,\\pi]$"},
  {id:"CN2.3.2-XR9", src:"Course notes 2.3, Exercise 9", lec:"L09", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 63",
   q:"$f(x) = \\sin(x)$ is invertible on $I = \\left[-\\frac\\pi2,\\frac\\pi2\\right]$. We denote this inverse by arcsin. What is the domain and range of arcsin?",
   sol:[
    "$\\sin(x)$ takes every value from $-1$ to $1$ for $-\\frac\\pi2\\le x\\le\\frac\\pi2$, so the domain of arcsin is $[-1,1]$.",
    "The range of arcsin is $I = \\left[-\\frac\\pi2,\\frac\\pi2\\right]$."],
   ans:"Domain $[-1,1]$, range $\\left[-\\frac\\pi2,\\frac\\pi2\\right]$"},
  {id:"CN2.3.2-XR10", src:"Course notes 2.3, Exercise 10", lec:"L09", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 64",
   q:"$f(x) = \\tan(x)$ is invertible on $I = \\left(-\\frac\\pi2,\\frac\\pi2\\right)$. We denote this inverse by arctan. What is the domain and range of arctan?",
   sol:[
    "$\\tan(x)$ takes every value in $(-\\infty,\\infty)$ for $-\\frac\\pi2<x<\\frac\\pi2$, so the domain of arctan is $(-\\infty,\\infty)$.",
    "The range of arctan is $I = \\left(-\\frac\\pi2,\\frac\\pi2\\right)$."],
   ans:"Domain $(-\\infty,\\infty)$, range $\\left(-\\frac\\pi2,\\frac\\pi2\\right)$"},
  {id:"CN2.3.2-EX10", src:"Course notes 2.3, Example 10", lec:"L09", kind:"example", page:"p. 64",
   q:"Verify that if $f(x) = 3x+5$, then $f^{-1}(y) = \\dfrac{y-5}3$.",
   sol:[
    "The domain of both $f$ and $f^{-1}$ is $(-\\infty,\\infty)$. Use the Cancellation Properties:",
    "$f^{-1}(f(x)) = f^{-1}(3x+5) = \\dfrac{(3x+5)-5}3 = x$",
    "$f(f^{-1}(y)) = f\\left(\\dfrac{y-5}3\\right) = 3\\left(\\dfrac{y-5}3\\right)+5 = y$",
    "So $f$ and $f^{-1}$ are inverses of each other."],
   ans:"Both compositions give the identity."},
  {id:"CN2.3.2-EX11", src:"Course notes 2.3, Example 11", lec:"L09", kind:"example", page:"p. 64",
   q:"Verify that if $f(x) = x^2$, then on $I = (-\\infty,0)$ we have $f^{-1}(y) = -\\sqrt y$.",
   sol:[
    "Restrict $f$ to $I = (-\\infty,0)$. The domain of $f^{-1}(y) = -\\sqrt y$ is $(0,\\infty)$.",
    "For any $x$ in $(-\\infty,0)$: $f^{-1}(f(x)) = f^{-1}(x^2) = -\\sqrt{x^2} = -|x| = -(-x) = x$",
    "For any $y$ in $(0,\\infty)$: $f(f^{-1}(y)) = f(-\\sqrt y) = (-\\sqrt y)^2 = y$",
    "So $f$ and $f^{-1}$ are inverses of each other."],
   ans:"Both compositions give the identity."},
  {id:"CN2.3.2-EX12", src:"Course notes 2.3, Example 12", lec:"L09", kind:"example", page:"p. 65",
   q:"Consider $f(x) = x^2$ on $I = (-\\infty,0)$. What is $f(f^{-1}(7))$? What is $f^{-1}(f(3))$?",
   sol:[
    "The domain of $f^{-1}$ is the range of $f$ on $I$, which is $(0,\\infty)$. Since 7 is in it, the Cancellation Properties give $f(f^{-1}(7)) = 7$.",
    "3 is not in $I$, so we cannot use the Cancellation Properties. Compute directly, using $f^{-1}(y) = -\\sqrt y$:",
    "$f^{-1}(f(3)) = f^{-1}(3^2) = f^{-1}(9) = -\\sqrt9 = -3$"],
   ans:"$7$ and $-3$"},
  {id:"CN2.3.2-EX13", src:"Course notes 2.3, Example 13", lec:"L09", kind:"example", page:"p. 65",
   q:"Given that $f(x) = \\tan(x)$ is invertible on $I = \\left(-\\frac\\pi2,\\frac\\pi2\\right)$, evaluate<br>(a) $f^{-1}\\left(f\\left(\\frac\\pi4\\right)\\right)$<br>(b) $f\\left(f^{-1}\\left(\\frac12\\right)\\right)$<br>(c) $f^{-1}\\left(f\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}4\\right)\\right)$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $\\frac\\pi4$ is in $I$, so by the Cancellation Properties $f^{-1}\\left(f\\left(\\frac\\pi4\\right)\\right) = \\frac\\pi4$.",
    "(b) The domain of $f^{-1}$ is the range of $\\tan$ on $I$, which is $(-\\infty,\\infty)$. So $\\frac12$ is in the domain of $f^{-1}$ and $f\\left(f^{-1}\\left(\\frac12\\right)\\right) = \\frac12$.",
    "(c) $\\frac{5\\pi}4$ is not in $I$, so evaluate directly: $\\tan\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}4\\right) = 1$, so $f^{-1}\\left(f\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}4\\right)\\right) = f^{-1}(1)$.",
    "$f^{-1}(1)$ is the angle $\\theta$ in $I$ with $\\tan(\\theta)=1$, which is $\\theta = \\frac\\pi4$."],
   ans:"(a) $\\frac\\pi4$; (b) $\\frac12$; (c) $\\frac\\pi4$",
   note:"In (c) the notes write $f^{-1}(-1)$ in the middle step, then evaluate $f^{-1}(1)$. Since $\\tan\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{4}\\right) = 1$, the middle step should read $f^{-1}(1)$. The final answer $\\frac\\pi4$ is correct."},
  {id:"CN2.3.2-XR11", src:"Course notes 2.3, Exercise 11", lec:"L09", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 66",
   q:"Given that $f(x) = \\sin(x)$ is invertible on $I = \\left[-\\frac\\pi2,\\frac\\pi2\\right]$, evaluate<br>(a) $f^{-1}\\left(f\\left(\\frac\\pi4\\right)\\right)$<br>(b) $f\\left(f^{-1}\\left(\\frac12\\right)\\right)$<br>(c) $f^{-1}\\left(f\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}4\\right)\\right)$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $\\frac\\pi4$ is in $I$, so $f^{-1}\\left(f\\left(\\frac\\pi4\\right)\\right) = \\frac\\pi4$.",
    "(b) $\\frac12$ is in the domain $[-1,1]$ of $f^{-1}$, so $f\\left(f^{-1}\\left(\\frac12\\right)\\right) = \\frac12$.",
    "(c) $\\frac{3\\pi}4$ is not in $I$, so evaluate directly: $f^{-1}\\left(\\sin\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}4\\right)\\right) = f^{-1}\\left(\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\right) = \\frac\\pi4$, since $\\frac\\pi4$ is in $I$ and $\\sin\\left(\\frac\\pi4\\right) = \\frac1{\\sqrt2}$."],
   ans:"(a) $\\frac\\pi4$; (b) $\\frac12$; (c) $\\frac\\pi4$"},
  {id:"CN2.3-P1", src:"Course notes 2.3, End of Section Problem 1", lec:"L09", kind:"problem", page:"p. 66",
   q:"Find the inverse of each function.<br>(a) $f(x) = 3x-2$<br>(b) $g(t) = 2\\sqrt{t-3}+1$<br>(c) $h(r) = r^3+5$<br>(d) $f(y) = -\\sqrt[3]{y+1}$<br>(e) $f(x) = \\dfrac{x+1}{x-2}$",
   sol:[
    "(a) Let $y = 3x-2$. Then $y+2 = 3x$, so $x = \\frac{y+2}3$.",
    "$f^{-1}(y) = \\dfrac{y+2}3$",
    "(b) Let $y = 2\\sqrt{t-3}+1$; note $y\\ge1$.",
    "$y-1 = 2\\sqrt{t-3} \\Rightarrow \\frac{y-1}2 = \\sqrt{t-3} \\Rightarrow \\left(\\frac{y-1}2\\right)^2 = t-3 \\Rightarrow t = \\left(\\frac{y-1}2\\right)^2+3$",
    "$g^{-1}(y) = \\left(\\dfrac{y-1}2\\right)^2+3$, for $y\\ge1$",
    "(c) Let $y = r^3+5$. Then $y-5 = r^3$, so $r = \\sqrt[3]{y-5}$.",
    "$h^{-1}(y) = \\sqrt[3]{y-5}$",
    "(d) Let $x = -\\sqrt[3]{y+1}$. Then $-x = \\sqrt[3]{y+1}$, so $(-x)^3 = y+1$, so $y = (-x)^3-1$.",
    "$f^{-1}(x) = (-x)^3-1$",
    "(e) Let $y = \\dfrac{x+1}{x-2} = \\dfrac{(x-2)+3}{x-2} = 1+\\dfrac3{x-2}$.",
    "$y-1 = \\dfrac3{x-2} \\Rightarrow x-2 = \\dfrac3{y-1} \\Rightarrow x = \\dfrac3{y-1}+2$",
    "$f^{-1}(y) = \\dfrac3{y-1}+2$"],
   ans:"(a) $f^{-1}(y)=\\frac{y+2}{3}$; (b) $g^{-1}(y)=\\left(\\frac{y-1}{2}\\right)^2+3$, $y\\ge1$; (c) $h^{-1}(y)=\\sqrt[3]{y-5}$; (d) $f^{-1}(x)=(-x)^3-1$; (e) $f^{-1}(y)=\\frac{3}{y-1}+2$"},
  {id:"CN2.3-P2", src:"Course notes 2.3, End of Section Problem 2", lec:"L09", kind:"problem", page:"p. 66",
   q:"Determine graphically whether the function has an inverse on its domain. If it does, find the inverse, $f^{-1}(y)$.<br>(a) $f(x) = \\sqrt{x+1}$<br>(b) $f(x) = x^2+2x+1$<br>(c) $f(x) = \\dfrac1x$<br>(d) $f(x) = \\tan(x)$<br>(e) $f(x) = |x|+2$",
   sol:[
    "(a) The graph starts at $(-1,0)$ and increases, so it passes the Horizontal Line Test and an inverse exists.",
    "Let $y = \\sqrt{x+1}$, $y\\ge0$. Then $y^2 = x+1$, so $x = y^2-1$.",
    "$f^{-1}(y) = y^2-1$, $y\\ge0$",
    "(b) $f(x) = (x+1)^2$ is a parabola with vertex $(-1,0)$. A horizontal line such as $y=1$ meets it twice ($x=0$ and $x=-2$), so it fails the Horizontal Line Test. No inverse.",
    "(c) The graph of $y=\\frac1x$ (a hyperbola) passes the Horizontal Line Test, so an inverse exists.",
    "Let $y = \\frac1x$. Then $yx = 1$, so $x = \\frac1y$.",
    "$f^{-1}(y) = \\dfrac1y$",
    "(d) The graph of $\\tan$ repeats on every interval of length $\\pi$, so for example $y=0$ meets it at $x=0,\\pm\\pi,\\ldots$ It fails the Horizontal Line Test. No inverse on its domain.",
    "(e) The graph is a V with vertex $(0,2)$. The line $y=3$ meets it at $x=\\pm1$, so it fails the Horizontal Line Test. No inverse."],
   ans:"(a) $f^{-1}(y)=y^2-1$, $y\\ge0$; (b) no inverse; (c) $f^{-1}(y)=\\frac1y$; (d) no inverse; (e) no inverse"},
  {id:"CN2.3-P3", src:"Course notes 2.3, End of Section Problem 3", lec:"L09", kind:"problem", page:"p. 66",
   q:"Find the inverse, $f^{-1}(y)$, of each function on the given interval. State the domain and the range of the inverse.<br>(a) $f(x) = x^2-1$ on $[0,\\infty)$<br>(b) $f(x) = x^2-1$ on $(-\\infty,0]$<br>(c) $f(x) = x+|x|$ on $[0,\\infty)$<br>(d) $f(x) = x^2-4x+4$ on $[3,\\infty)$<br>(e) $f(x) = \\dfrac1{x^4}$ on $(-\\infty,-1]$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $y = x^2-1 \\Rightarrow y+1 = x^2 \\Rightarrow \\sqrt{y+1} = |x| = x$ since $x\\ge0$.",
    "$f^{-1}(y) = \\sqrt{y+1}$",
    "For $0\\le x<\\infty$, $-1\\le f(x)<\\infty$, so the domain of $f^{-1}$ is $[-1,\\infty)$. The range of $f^{-1}$ is $[0,\\infty)$.",
    "(b) As in (a), $\\sqrt{y+1} = |x| = -x$ since $x\\le0$, so $x = -\\sqrt{y+1}$.",
    "$f^{-1}(y) = -\\sqrt{y+1}$",
    "For $-\\infty<x\\le0$, $-1\\le f(x)<\\infty$, so the domain of $f^{-1}$ is $[-1,\\infty)$. The range of $f^{-1}$ is $(-\\infty,0]$.",
    "(c) Since $x\\ge0$, $f(x) = x+x = 2x$. $y = 2x \\Rightarrow x = \\frac y2$.",
    "$f^{-1}(y) = \\dfrac y2$",
    "For $0\\le x<\\infty$, $0\\le f(x)<\\infty$, so the domain of $f^{-1}$ is $[0,\\infty)$. The range is $[0,\\infty)$.",
    "(d) $y = (x-2)^2 \\Rightarrow \\sqrt y = |x-2| = x-2$ since $x\\ge3$, so $x = \\sqrt y+2$.",
    "$f^{-1}(y) = \\sqrt y+2$",
    "For $3\\le x<\\infty$, $1\\le f(x)<\\infty$, so the domain of $f^{-1}$ is $[1,\\infty)$. The range is $[3,\\infty)$.",
    "(e) $y = \\frac1{x^4} \\Rightarrow x^4 = \\frac1y \\Rightarrow |x| = \\sqrt[4]{\\frac1y} \\Rightarrow -x = \\sqrt[4]{\\frac1y}$ since $x\\le-1$.",
    "$f^{-1}(y) = -\\sqrt[4]{\\dfrac1y}$",
    "For $-\\infty<x\\le-1$, $0<f(x)\\le1$, so the domain of $f^{-1}$ is $(0,1]$. The range is $(-\\infty,-1]$."],
   ans:"(a) $\\sqrt{y+1}$; domain $[-1,\\infty)$, range $[0,\\infty)$; (b) $-\\sqrt{y+1}$; domain $[-1,\\infty)$, range $(-\\infty,0]$; (c) $\\frac y2$; domain $[0,\\infty)$, range $[0,\\infty)$; (d) $\\sqrt y+2$; domain $[1,\\infty)$, range $[3,\\infty)$; (e) $-\\sqrt[4]{\\frac1y}$; domain $(0,1]$, range $(-\\infty,-1]$"},
  {id:"CN2.3-P4", src:"Course notes 2.3, End of Section Problem 4", lec:"L09", kind:"problem", page:"p. 66",
   q:"Split the domain of $f$ into two intervals such that $f$ has an inverse on each interval. Find the inverse, $f^{-1}(y)$, for each interval.<br>(a) $f(x) = x^4$<br>(b) $f(x) = x^2+2x+3$<br>(c) $f(x) = |x^5-10|$",
   sol:[
    "(a) Split the domain into $(-\\infty,0)$ and $[0,\\infty)$. Let $y=x^4$, so $\\sqrt[4]y = \\sqrt[4]{x^4} = |x|$.",
    "On $(-\\infty,0)$: $|x|=-x$, so $\\sqrt[4]y = -x$ and $f^{-1}(y) = -\\sqrt[4]y$.",
    "On $[0,\\infty)$: $|x|=x$, so $f^{-1}(y) = \\sqrt[4]y$.",
    "(b) Complete the square: $f(x) = (x+1)^2+2$. Split into $(-\\infty,-1)$ and $[-1,\\infty)$.",
    "$y = (x+1)^2+2 \\Rightarrow y-2 = (x+1)^2 \\Rightarrow \\sqrt{y-2} = |x+1|$",
    "On $(-\\infty,-1)$: $|x+1| = -(x+1)$, so $x = -\\sqrt{y-2}-1$ and $f^{-1}(y) = -\\sqrt{y-2}-1$.",
    "On $[-1,\\infty)$: $|x+1| = x+1$, so $f^{-1}(y) = \\sqrt{y-2}-1$.",
    "(c) The transition point is where $x^5-10=0$, that is $x = \\sqrt[5]{10}$. Split into $(-\\infty,\\sqrt[5]{10})$ and $[\\sqrt[5]{10},\\infty)$.",
    "On $(-\\infty,\\sqrt[5]{10})$: $x^5-10<0$, so $y = -(x^5-10) = 10-x^5$, so $x^5 = 10-y$ and $f^{-1}(y) = \\sqrt[5]{10-y}$.",
    "On $[\\sqrt[5]{10},\\infty)$: $x^5-10\\ge0$, so $y = x^5-10$, so $x^5 = y+10$ and $f^{-1}(y) = \\sqrt[5]{y+10}$."],
   ans:"(a) $-\\sqrt[4]y$ on $(-\\infty,0)$; $\\sqrt[4]y$ on $[0,\\infty)$; (b) $-\\sqrt{y-2}-1$ on $(-\\infty,-1)$; $\\sqrt{y-2}-1$ on $[-1,\\infty)$; (c) $\\sqrt[5]{10-y}$ on $(-\\infty,\\sqrt[5]{10})$; $\\sqrt[5]{y+10}$ on $[\\sqrt[5]{10},\\infty)$"},
  {id:"CN2.3-P5-a-d", src:"Course notes 2.3, End of Section Problem 5, parts (a) to (d)", lec:"L09", kind:"problem", page:"p. 66",
   q:"Assuming the function is invertible on the given interval, find the specified quantities.<br>(a) $f(x) = x^3+x$ on $(-\\infty,\\infty)$; $f^{-1}(0)$, $f^{-1}(10)$<br>(b) $g(x) = 3+x+e^x$ on $(-\\infty,\\infty)$; $g^{-1}(4)$, $g^{-1}(4+e)$<br>(c) $f(x) = \\sqrt{x+1}$ on $[-1,\\infty)$; $f^{-1}(2)$, $f^{-1}(9)$<br>(d) $h(x) = \\frac13x^3+x-1$ on $(-\\infty,\\infty)$; $h^{-1}(-1)$, $h^{-1}\\left(-\\frac73\\right)$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $f(0) = 0$, so $f^{-1}(0)=0$.",
    "$f(2) = 8+2 = 10$, so $f^{-1}(10)=2$.",
    "(b) $g(0) = 3+0+1 = 4$, so $g^{-1}(4)=0$.",
    "$g(1) = 3+1+e = 4+e$, so $g^{-1}(4+e)=1$.",
    "(c) $\\sqrt{x+1} = 2 \\Rightarrow x+1 = 4 \\Rightarrow x=3$, so $f^{-1}(2)=3$.",
    "$\\sqrt{x+1} = 9 \\Rightarrow x+1 = 81 \\Rightarrow x=80$, so $f^{-1}(9)=80$.",
    "(d) $h(0) = -1$, so $h^{-1}(-1)=0$.",
    "$h(-1) = -\\frac13-1-1 = -\\frac73$, so $h^{-1}\\left(-\\frac73\\right)=-1$."],
   ans:"(a) $0$, $2$; (b) $0$, $1$; (c) $3$, $80$; (d) $0$, $-1$",
   note:"The official solutions give only the final answers; the working is added here."},
  {id:"CN2.3-P5-e-h", src:"Course notes 2.3, End of Section Problem 5, parts (e) to (h)", lec:"L09", kind:"problem", page:"p. 66",
   q:"Assuming the function is invertible on the given interval, find the specified quantities.<br>(e) $f(x) = x^2$ on $[0,\\infty)$; $f^{-1}(1)$, $f^{-1}(3)$<br>(f) $f(x) = x^2$ on $(-\\infty,0]$; $f^{-1}(1)$, $f^{-1}(3)$<br>(g) $f(x) = \\sin(x)$ on $\\left[-\\frac\\pi2,\\frac\\pi2\\right]$; $f^{-1}(0)$, $f^{-1}\\left(\\frac12\\right)$<br>(h) $f(x) = \\sin(x)$ on $\\left[\\frac\\pi2,\\frac{3\\pi}2\\right]$; $f^{-1}(0)$, $f^{-1}\\left(\\frac12\\right)$",
   sol:[
    "(e) We need $x\\ge0$: $x^2=1$ gives $x=1$, and $x^2=3$ gives $x=\\sqrt3$.",
    "$f^{-1}(1)=1$, $f^{-1}(3)=\\sqrt3$",
    "(f) We need $x\\le0$: $x^2=1$ gives $x=-1$, and $x^2=3$ gives $x=-\\sqrt3$.",
    "$f^{-1}(1)=-1$, $f^{-1}(3)=-\\sqrt3$",
    "(g) In $\\left[-\\frac\\pi2,\\frac\\pi2\\right]$: $\\sin(0)=0$ and $\\sin\\left(\\frac\\pi6\\right)=\\frac12$.",
    "$f^{-1}(0)=0$, $f^{-1}\\left(\\frac12\\right)=\\frac\\pi6$",
    "(h) In $\\left[\\frac\\pi2,\\frac{3\\pi}2\\right]$: $\\sin(\\pi)=0$ and $\\sin\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}6\\right)=\\frac12$.",
    "$f^{-1}(0)=\\pi$, $f^{-1}\\left(\\frac12\\right)=\\frac{5\\pi}6$"],
   ans:"(e) $1$, $\\sqrt3$; (f) $-1$, $-\\sqrt3$; (g) $0$, $\\frac\\pi6$; (h) $\\pi$, $\\frac{5\\pi}{6}$",
   note:"The official solutions give only the final answers; the working is added here."},
  {id:"CN2.3-P5-i-j", src:"Course notes 2.3, End of Section Problem 5, parts (i) to (j)", lec:"L09", kind:"problem", page:"p. 66",
   q:"Assuming the function is invertible on the given interval, find the specified quantities.<br>(i) $f(x) = \\cos(x)$ on $[0,\\pi]$; $f^{-1}(-1)$, $f^{-1}\\left(\\frac12\\right)$<br>(j) $f(x) = \\cos(x)$ on $[\\pi,2\\pi]$; $f^{-1}(-1)$, $f^{-1}\\left(\\frac12\\right)$",
   sol:[
    "(i) In $[0,\\pi]$: $\\cos(\\pi)=-1$ and $\\cos\\left(\\frac\\pi3\\right)=\\frac12$.",
    "$f^{-1}(-1)=\\pi$, $f^{-1}\\left(\\frac12\\right)=\\frac\\pi3$",
    "(j) In $[\\pi,2\\pi]$: $\\cos(\\pi)=-1$ and $\\cos\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}3\\right)=\\frac12$.",
    "$f^{-1}(-1)=\\pi$, $f^{-1}\\left(\\frac12\\right)=\\frac{5\\pi}3$"],
   ans:"(i) $\\pi$, $\\frac\\pi3$; (j) $\\pi$, $\\frac{5\\pi}{3}$",
   note:"The official solutions give only the final answers; the working is added here."},
  {id:"CN2.3-P6", src:"Course notes 2.3, End of Section Problem 6", lec:"L09", kind:"problem", page:"p. 66",
   q:"Let $q = f(p)$, where $p$ is the price of an item in dollars and $q$ is the number of items sold at that price. What is the physical meaning of (a) $f(10)$ (b) $f^{-1}(5)$?",
   sol:[
    "(a) $f(10)$ is the number of items sold when the price is 10 dollars.",
    "(b) $f^{-1}$ takes a number of items and returns a price, so $f^{-1}(5)$ is the price at which 5 items are sold."],
   ans:"(a) items sold at \\$10; (b) the price at which 5 items are sold"},
  {id:"CN2.3-P7", src:"Course notes 2.3, End of Section Problem 7", lec:"L09", kind:"problem", page:"p. 66",
   q:"Let $C = C(A)$ be the cost, in thousands of dollars, of building a house of area $A$ square feet. What is the physical meaning of (a) $C(1000)$ (b) $C^{-1}(1000)$?",
   sol:[
    "(a) $C(1000)$ is the cost (in thousands of dollars) of building a house with an area of 1000 square feet.",
    "(b) $C^{-1}(1000)$ is the area, in square feet, of a house that costs 1000 thousand dollars, that is \\$1,000,000."],
   ans:"(a) cost of a 1000 ft$^2$ house; (b) area of a house costing \\$1,000,000"},
  {id:"CN2.3-P8", src:"Course notes 2.3, End of Section Problem 8", lec:"L09", kind:"problem", page:"p. 67",
   q:"fMRI determines brain activity from changes in blood flow to parts of the brain. Blood flow $F$ is linearly related to brain activity $x$.<br>(a) Use the table to determine a linear function $F(x)$ for blood flow in terms of brain activity.<table class=\"cn-table\"><tr><th>$x$</th><td>0.16</td><td>1.0</td></tr><tr><th>$F$</th><td>0.52</td><td>1.0</td></tr></table>(b) Find $F^{-1}$ and state what it represents.",
   sol:[
    "(a) Slope: $m = \\dfrac{1-0.52}{1-0.16} = \\dfrac{0.48}{0.84} = \\dfrac47$.",
    "Use the point $(1,1)$ in $F = mx+b$: $1 = \\frac47(1)+b$, so $b = \\frac37$.",
    "$F(x) = \\frac47x+\\frac37$",
    "(b) $F = \\frac47x+\\frac37 \\Rightarrow F-\\frac37 = \\frac47x \\Rightarrow x = \\frac74\\left(F-\\frac37\\right) = \\frac{7F-3}4$",
    "$F^{-1}(F) = \\dfrac{7F-3}4$. It gives the brain activity for a given blood flow."],
   ans:"(a) $F(x)=\\frac47x+\\frac37$; (b) $F^{-1}(F)=\\frac{7F-3}{4}$, brain activity from blood flow"},
  {id:"CN2.3-P9", src:"Course notes 2.3, End of Section Problem 9", lec:"L09", kind:"problem", page:"p. 67",
   q:"Bacteria are growing in a culture. The time $t$ (in hours) for the number of bacteria to double depends on the temperature $T$ (in °C) of the culture by $t = f(T) = \\begin{cases} \\frac1{24}T+\\frac{11}4 & \\text{if } 30\\le T\\le36 \\\\ \\frac43T-\\frac{175}4 & \\text{if } 36<T\\le39\\end{cases}$<br>(a) How many hours does it take the bacteria to double at 31°C?<br>(b) Find the piecewise inverse function $T = f^{-1}(t)$.<br>(c) Use (b) to find the temperature at which the bacteria double in 8 hours.",
   sol:[
    "(a) 31 is in $30\\le T\\le36$, so $f(31) = \\frac{31}{24}+\\frac{11}4 = \\frac{97}{24}$ hours.",
    "(b) For $30\\le T\\le36$: $t = \\frac1{24}T+\\frac{11}4 \\Rightarrow T = 24\\left(t-\\frac{11}4\\right)$. Here $f(30)=4$ and $f(36)=\\frac{17}4$, so this piece has $4\\le t\\le\\frac{17}4$.",
    "For $36<T\\le39$: $t = \\frac43T-\\frac{175}4 \\Rightarrow T = \\frac34\\left(t+\\frac{175}4\\right)$. Here $\\frac{17}4<f(T)\\le\\frac{33}4$, so this piece has $\\frac{17}4<t\\le\\frac{33}4$.",
    "$f^{-1}(t) = \\begin{cases} 24\\left(t-\\frac{11}4\\right) & \\text{if } 4\\le t\\le\\frac{17}4 \\\\ \\frac34\\left(t+\\frac{175}4\\right) & \\text{if } \\frac{17}4<t\\le\\frac{33}4\\end{cases}$",
    "(c) $t=8$ satisfies $\\frac{17}4<8\\le\\frac{33}4$, so $f^{-1}(8) = \\frac34\\left(8+\\frac{175}4\\right) = \\frac34\\cdot\\frac{207}4 = \\frac{621}{16}$",
    "The bacteria double in 8 hours at $\\frac{621}{16}\\approx 38.8$°C."],
   ans:"(a) $\\frac{97}{24}$ h; (b) see the piecewise formula; (c) $\\frac{621}{16}$°C"},
  {id:"CN2.3-P10", src:"Course notes 2.3, End of Section Problem 10", lec:"L09", kind:"problem", page:"p. 67",
   q:"Given that $f(x) = x^2$ is invertible on $I = [0,\\infty)$, what is<br>(a) $f(f^{-1}(2))$<br>(b) $f^{-1}(f(5))$<br>(c) $f^{-1}(f(-3))$",
   sol:[
    "(a) 2 is in the domain $[0,\\infty)$ of $f^{-1}$, so $f(f^{-1}(2)) = 2$.",
    "(b) 5 is in $I$, so $f^{-1}(f(5)) = 5$.",
    "(c) $-3$ is not in $I$, so evaluate directly: $f^{-1}(f(-3)) = f^{-1}(9) = \\sqrt9 = 3$."],
   ans:"(a) $2$; (b) $5$; (c) $3$"},
  {id:"CN2.3-P11", src:"Course notes 2.3, End of Section Problem 11", lec:"L09", kind:"problem", page:"p. 67",
   q:"Given that $f(x)=x^2$ is invertible on $I = (-\\infty,0]$, what is<br>(a) $f(f^{-1}(-2))$<br>(b) $f^{-1}(f(5))$<br>(c) $f^{-1}(f(-3))$",
   sol:[
    "(a) The domain of $f^{-1}$ is $[0,\\infty)$, so $-2$ is not in it. The expression is undefined.",
    "(b) 5 is not in $I$, so evaluate directly: $f^{-1}(f(5)) = f^{-1}(25) = -\\sqrt{25} = -5$.",
    "(c) $-3$ is in $I$, so $f^{-1}(f(-3)) = -3$."],
   ans:"(a) undefined; (b) $-5$; (c) $-3$"},
  {id:"CN2.3-P12", src:"Course notes 2.3, End of Section Problem 12", lec:"L09", kind:"problem", page:"p. 67",
   q:"Given that $f(x) = \\cos(x)$ is invertible on $I = [0,\\pi]$, what is<br>(a) $f(f^{-1}(1))$<br>(b) $f\\left(f^{-1}\\left(\\frac12\\right)\\right)$<br>(c) $f^{-1}(f(0))$<br>(d) $f^{-1}\\left(f\\left(\\frac\\pi4\\right)\\right)$<br>(e) $f^{-1}(f(-\\pi))$",
   sol:[
    "The domain of $f^{-1}$ is $[-1,1]$.",
    "(a) 1 is in the domain of $f^{-1}$, so $f(f^{-1}(1)) = 1$.",
    "(b) $\\frac12$ is in the domain of $f^{-1}$, so $f\\left(f^{-1}\\left(\\frac12\\right)\\right) = \\frac12$.",
    "(c) 0 is in $I$, so $f^{-1}(f(0)) = 0$.",
    "(d) $\\frac\\pi4$ is in $I$, so $f^{-1}\\left(f\\left(\\frac\\pi4\\right)\\right) = \\frac\\pi4$.",
    "(e) $-\\pi$ is not in $I$, so evaluate directly: $f^{-1}(f(-\\pi)) = f^{-1}(\\cos(-\\pi)) = f^{-1}(-1) = \\pi$."],
   ans:"(a) $1$; (b) $\\frac12$; (c) $0$; (d) $\\frac\\pi4$; (e) $\\pi$"},
  {id:"CN2.4.1-EX1", src:"Course notes 2.4, Example 1", lec:"L10", kind:"example", page:"p. 68",
   q:"Let $f(x) = 2^x$. Evaluate $f(3)$, $f(1)$, $f(0)$ and $f(-4)$.",
   sol:[
    "$f(3) = 2^3 = 8$, $f(1) = 2^1 = 2$, $f(0) = 2^0 = 1$, $f(-4) = 2^{-4} = \\frac1{16}$"],
   ans:"$8$, $2$, $1$, $\\frac1{16}$"},
  {id:"CN2.4.1-XR1", src:"Course notes 2.4, Exercise 1", lec:"L10", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 68",
   q:"Let $f(x) = \\left(\\frac13\\right)^x$. Find $f(2)$, $f(0)$ and $f(-3)$.",
   sol:[
    "$f(2) = \\left(\\frac13\\right)^2 = \\frac19$",
    "$f(0) = \\left(\\frac13\\right)^0 = 1$",
    "$f(-3) = \\left(\\frac13\\right)^{-3} = 3^3 = 27$"],
   ans:"$\\frac19$, $1$, $27$"},
  {id:"CN2.4.2-EX2", src:"Course notes 2.4, Example 2", lec:"L10", kind:"example", page:"p. 69",
   q:"Determine the derivative of $f(x) = 2^x$.",
   sol:[
    "Using $\\frac{d}{dx}a^x = a^x\\ln(a)$: $f'(x) = 2^x\\ln(2)$"],
   ans:"$f'(x) = 2^x\\ln(2)$"},
  {id:"CN2.4.2-XR2", src:"Course notes 2.4, Exercise 2", lec:"L10", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 69",
   q:"Determine the second derivative of $f(x) = x3^x$.",
   sol:[
    "By the Product Rule, $f'(x) = 3^x + x3^x\\ln(3)$",
    "$f''(x) = 3^x\\ln(3) + \\left[3^x\\ln(3) + x3^x(\\ln(3))^2\\right] = 2\\ln(3)\\,3^x + x3^x(\\ln(3))^2$"],
   ans:"$f''(x) = 2\\ln(3)3^x + x3^x(\\ln 3)^2$"},
  {id:"CN2.4.2-EX3", src:"Course notes 2.4, Example 3", lec:"L10", kind:"example", page:"p. 70",
   q:"Determine $\\displaystyle\\int5^x\\,dx$.",
   sol:[
    "Using $\\int a^x\\,dx = \\frac{a^x}{\\ln(a)}+C$: $\\int5^x\\,dx = \\dfrac{5^x}{\\ln(5)}+C$"],
   ans:"$\\frac{5^x}{\\ln(5)}+C$"},
  {id:"CN2.4.2-XR3", src:"Course notes 2.4, Exercise 3", lec:"L10", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 70",
   q:"Determine $\\displaystyle\\int4^x\\,dx$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\int4^x\\,dx = \\dfrac{4^x}{\\ln(4)}+C$"],
   ans:"$\\frac{4^x}{\\ln(4)}+C$"},
  {id:"CN2.4.2-EX4", src:"Course notes 2.4, Example 4", lec:"L10", kind:"example", page:"p. 70",
   q:"Find all solutions of the first order differential equation $y' = 3^x+x$.",
   sol:[
    "$y = \\int(3^x+x)\\,dx = \\dfrac{3^x}{\\ln(3)}+\\dfrac12x^2+C$"],
   ans:"$y = \\frac{3^x}{\\ln(3)}+\\frac12x^2+C$"},
  {id:"CN2.4.2-XR4", src:"Course notes 2.4, Exercise 4", lec:"L10", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 70",
   q:"Solve the initial value problem $\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = 2^x$, $y(1) = 2$.",
   sol:[
    "All solutions: $y = \\int2^x\\,dx = \\dfrac{2^x}{\\ln(2)}+C$",
    "$y=2$ when $x=1$: $2 = \\dfrac{2}{\\ln(2)}+C$, so $C = 2-\\dfrac2{\\ln(2)}$",
    "$y = \\dfrac{2^x}{\\ln(2)}+2-\\dfrac2{\\ln(2)}$"],
   ans:"$y = \\frac{2^x}{\\ln(2)}+2-\\frac{2}{\\ln(2)}$"},
  {id:"CN2.4.3-EX5", src:"Course notes 2.4, Example 5", lec:"L10", kind:"example", page:"p. 71",
   q:"Evaluate<br>(a) $\\log_3(9)$<br>(b) $\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac1{100}\\right)$<br>(c) $\\log_3(729)$<br>(d) $\\log_2(4^{2/3})$<br>(e) $e^{2\\ln(5)}$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $\\log_3(9)$ asks for $y$ with $3^y = 9$. Since $3^2=9$, $\\log_3(9) = 2$.",
    "(b) We need $10^y = \\frac1{100}$. Since $10^{-2} = \\frac1{100}$, $\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac1{100}\\right) = -2$.",
    "(c) $\\log_3(729) = \\log_3(9\\cdot81) = \\log_3(3\\cdot3\\cdot3^4) = \\log_3(3)+\\log_3(3)+\\log_3(3^4) = 1+1+4 = 6$",
    "(d) $\\log_2(4^{2/3}) = \\log_2\\left((2^2)^{2/3}\\right) = \\log_2(2^{4/3}) = \\frac43\\log_2(2) = \\frac43$",
    "(e) Move the coefficient inside first: $e^{2\\ln(5)} = e^{\\ln(5^2)} = 5^2 = 25$"],
   ans:"(a) $2$; (b) $-2$; (c) $6$; (d) $\\frac43$; (e) $25$"},
  {id:"CN2.4.3-XR5", src:"Course notes 2.4, Exercise 5", lec:"L10", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 72",
   q:"Evaluate.<br>(a) $\\log_4(64)$<br>(b) $\\log_{10}(1000)$<br>(c) $\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac1{1000000}\\right)$<br>(d) $3^{\\log_3(5)}$<br>(e) $2^{3\\log_2(7)}$<br>(f) $\\ln(e^2+e^3)$",
   sol:[
    "(a) Since $4^3 = 64$, $\\log_4(64) = 3$.",
    "(b) Since $10^3 = 1000$, $\\log_{10}(1000) = 3$.",
    "(c) Since $10^{-6} = \\frac1{1000000}$, $\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac1{1000000}\\right) = -6$.",
    "(d) By $a^{\\log_a(x)} = x$, $3^{\\log_3(5)} = 5$.",
    "(e) $2^{3\\log_2(7)} = 2^{\\log_2(7^3)} = 7^3 = 343$",
    "(f) $\\ln(e^2+e^3) = \\ln\\left(e^2(1+e)\\right) = \\ln(e^2)+\\ln(1+e) = 2+\\ln(1+e)$"],
   ans:"(a) $3$; (b) $3$; (c) $-6$; (d) $5$; (e) $7^3=343$; (f) $2+\\ln(1+e)$"},
  {id:"CN2.4.3-XR6", src:"Course notes 2.4, Exercise 6", lec:"L10", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 72",
   q:"Express $\\ln(a+b)+\\ln(a-b)-2\\ln(c)$ as a single logarithm.",
   sol:[
    "$\\ln(a+b)+\\ln(a-b)-2\\ln(c) = \\ln[(a+b)(a-b)]-\\ln(c^2) = \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{a^2-b^2}{c^2}\\right)$"],
   ans:"$\\ln\\left(\\frac{a^2-b^2}{c^2}\\right)$"},
  {id:"CN2.4.3-EX6", src:"Course notes 2.4, Example 6", lec:"L10", kind:"example", page:"p. 72",
   q:"Let $f(x) = \\dfrac{(x-1)(x+2)^2}{(x+3)^3(x+1)}$. Use the properties of logarithms to write $\\ln(f(x))$ as a sum and difference of functions.",
   sol:[
    "$\\ln(f(x)) = \\ln\\left((x-1)(x+2)^2\\right) - \\ln\\left((x+3)^3(x+1)\\right)$",
    "$= \\ln(x-1)+\\ln\\left((x+2)^2\\right) - \\left[\\ln\\left((x+3)^3\\right)+\\ln(x+1)\\right]$",
    "$= \\ln(x-1)+2\\ln(x+2)-3\\ln(x+3)-\\ln(x+1)$"],
   ans:"$\\ln(x-1)+2\\ln(x+2)-3\\ln(x+3)-\\ln(x+1)$"},
  {id:"CN2.4.3-XR7", src:"Course notes 2.4, Exercise 7", lec:"L10", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 72",
   q:"Let $f(x) = \\dfrac{(x-2)^5}{x^2(x+1)^3}$. Use the properties of logarithms to write $\\ln(f(x))$ as a sum and difference of functions.",
   sol:[
    "$\\ln(f(x)) = \\ln((x-2)^5)-\\ln(x^2(x+1)^3) = 5\\ln(x-2)-\\left[\\ln(x^2)+\\ln((x+1)^3)\\right]$",
    "$= 5\\ln(x-2)-2\\ln(x)-3\\ln(x+1)$"],
   ans:"$5\\ln(x-2)-2\\ln(x)-3\\ln(x+1)$"},
  {id:"CN2.4.3-XR8", src:"Course notes 2.4, Exercise 8", lec:"L10", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 72",
   q:"Suppose $f(x) = \\dfrac1{1+e^{-(b+mx)}}$, where $b$ and $m$ are constants (a logistic function). Show that $\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{f(x)}{1-f(x)}\\right) = b+mx$.",
   sol:[
    "Write $u = e^{-(b+mx)}$, so $f(x) = \\frac1{1+u}$ and $1-f(x) = \\frac{1+u-1}{1+u} = \\frac{u}{1+u}$.",
    "$\\dfrac{f(x)}{1-f(x)} = \\dfrac{\\frac1{1+u}}{\\frac{u}{1+u}} = \\dfrac1u = \\dfrac1{e^{-(b+mx)}} = e^{b+mx}$",
    "So $\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{f(x)}{1-f(x)}\\right) = \\ln\\left(e^{b+mx}\\right) = b+mx$."],
   ans:"$\\ln\\left(\\frac{f}{1-f}\\right) = b+mx$"},
  {id:"CN2.4.3-EX7", src:"Course notes 2.4, Example 7", lec:"L10", kind:"example", page:"p. 73",
   q:"A dead body is found at 12 pm in a room kept at 20°C. The body is 28°C when found and has cooled to 27°C at 1 pm. The temperature of the body satisfies $T(t) = 20+Ae^{kt}$, and the temperature at the time of death was 37°C. Estimate the time of death.",
   sol:[
    "Let $t=0$ be 12 pm, when the body is found. Then $T(0) = 28$ and $T(1) = 27$.",
    "$T(0)=28$: $28 = 20+Ae^0$, so $A = 8$.",
    "$T(1)=27$: $27 = 20+8e^k \\Rightarrow \\frac78 = e^k \\Rightarrow k = \\ln\\left(\\frac78\\right)$",
    "So $T(t) = 20+8e^{\\ln(7/8)t}$. Set $T(t) = 37$:",
    "$37 = 20+8e^{\\ln(7/8)t} \\Rightarrow \\frac{17}8 = e^{\\ln(7/8)t} \\Rightarrow \\ln\\left(\\frac{17}8\\right) = \\ln\\left(\\frac78\\right)t \\Rightarrow t = \\dfrac{\\ln(17/8)}{\\ln(7/8)}$",
    "$t\\approx -5.6$, so death occurred about 5.6 hours before 12 pm, at about 6:20 am."],
   ans:"$t = \\frac{\\ln(17/8)}{\\ln(7/8)}\\approx-5.6$ h, about 6:20 am",
   note:"The notes round $t$ to $-6$ and give 6:00 am. More precisely $t\\approx-5.645$ h, which is about 6:21 am."},
  {id:"CN2.4.3-EX8", src:"Course notes 2.4, Example 8", lec:"L10", kind:"example", page:"p. 74",
   q:"Approximate $\\ln(2)$ using the left Riemann sum with $n=4$ subdivisions.",
   sol:[
    "By definition $\\ln(2) = \\int_1^2\\frac1t\\,dt$.",
    "$\\Delta t = \\frac{2-1}4 = \\frac14$ and $t_i = 1+\\frac i4$ for $0\\le i\\le3$.",
    "$\\ln(2)\\approx\\frac11\\Delta t+\\frac1{5/4}\\Delta t+\\frac1{3/2}\\Delta t+\\frac1{7/4}\\Delta t = \\frac14+\\frac15+\\frac16+\\frac17 = \\frac{319}{420}$"],
   ans:"$\\ln(2)\\approx\\frac{319}{420}$"},
  {id:"CN2.4.3-XR9", src:"Course notes 2.4, Exercise 9", lec:"L10", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 74",
   q:"Approximate $\\ln(3)$ using the left Riemann sum with $n=4$ subdivisions.",
   sol:[
    "By definition $\\ln(3) = \\int_1^3\\frac1t\\,dt$.",
    "$\\Delta t = \\frac{3-1}4 = \\frac12$ and $t_i = 1+\\frac i2$ for $0\\le i\\le3$.",
    "$\\ln(3)\\approx\\frac11\\Delta t+\\frac1{1.5}\\Delta t+\\frac12\\Delta t+\\frac1{2.5}\\Delta t = 1\\cdot\\frac12+\\frac23\\cdot\\frac12+\\frac12\\cdot\\frac12+\\frac25\\cdot\\frac12 = \\frac{77}{60}$"],
   ans:"$\\ln(3)\\approx\\frac{77}{60}$"},
  {id:"CN2.4.4-EX9", src:"Course notes 2.4, Example 9", lec:"L11", kind:"example", page:"p. 76",
   q:"Determine the derivative of $f(x) = x\\ln(x)-x$.",
   sol:[
    "By the Product Rule, $f'(x) = 1\\cdot\\ln(x)+x\\cdot\\frac1x-1 = \\ln(x)$"],
   ans:"$f'(x) = \\ln(x)$"},
  {id:"CN2.4.4-EX10", src:"Course notes 2.4, Example 10", lec:"L11", kind:"example", page:"p. 76",
   q:"Determine the derivative of $f(x) = x^2\\ln(2x)$.",
   sol:[
    "By the Product Rule and the Chain Rule, $f'(x) = 2x\\ln(2x)+x^2\\cdot\\frac1{2x}\\cdot2 = 2x\\ln(2x)+x$"],
   ans:"$f'(x) = 2x\\ln(2x)+x$"},
  {id:"CN2.4.4-XR10", src:"Course notes 2.4, Exercise 10", lec:"L11", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 76",
   q:"Determine the derivative of $f(x) = x^2\\ln(2x)$ by first using $\\ln(2x) = \\ln(2)+\\ln(x)$ and then taking the derivative.",
   sol:[
    "$f(x) = x^2(\\ln(2)+\\ln(x)) = \\ln(2)x^2+x^2\\ln(x)$",
    "$f'(x) = 2\\ln(2)x+\\left(2x\\ln(x)+x^2\\cdot\\frac1x\\right) = 2\\ln(2)x+2x\\ln(x)+x$"],
   ans:"$f'(x) = 2\\ln(2)x+2x\\ln(x)+x$"},
  {id:"CN2.4.4-XR11", src:"Course notes 2.4, Exercise 11", lec:"L11", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 76",
   q:"Verify that $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\ln(|x|) = \\dfrac1x$.",
   sol:[
    "By the Chain Rule, $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\ln(|x|) = \\dfrac1{|x|}\\cdot\\dfrac{x}{|x|} = \\dfrac{x}{|x|^2} = \\dfrac{x}{(\\sqrt{x^2})^2} = \\dfrac{x}{x^2} = \\dfrac1x$"],
   ans:"$\\frac{d}{dx}\\ln|x| = \\frac1x$"},
  {id:"CN2.4.4-EX11", src:"Course notes 2.4, Example 11", lec:"L11", kind:"example", page:"p. 77",
   q:"Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int\\left(x+1+\\frac1x+\\frac1{x^2}\\right)dx$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\int\\left(x+1+\\frac1x+x^{-2}\\right)dx = \\frac12x^2+x+\\ln(|x|)-\\frac1x+C$"],
   ans:"$\\frac12x^2+x+\\ln|x|-\\frac1x+C$"},
  {id:"CN2.4.4-XR12", src:"Course notes 2.4, Exercise 12", lec:"L11", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 77",
   q:"Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int\\left(e^x+\\frac1x\\right)dx$.",
   sol:[
    "$\\int\\left(e^x+\\frac1x\\right)dx = e^x+\\ln(|x|)+C$"],
   ans:"$e^x+\\ln|x|+C$"},
  {id:"CN2.4.4-EX12", src:"Course notes 2.4, Example 12", lec:"L11", kind:"example", page:"p. 77",
   q:"Solve the initial value problem $y' = \\dfrac1x$, $y(1) = 2$.",
   sol:[
    "$y = \\int\\frac1x\\,dx = \\ln(|x|)+C$",
    "$y=2$ when $x=1$: $2 = \\ln(|1|)+C = 0+C$, so $C = 2$.",
    "$y(x) = \\ln(|x|)+2$"],
   ans:"$y = \\ln|x|+2$"},
  {id:"CN2.4.4-XR13", src:"Course notes 2.4, Exercise 13", lec:"L11", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 77",
   q:"Solve the initial value problem $y' = \\dfrac1{x\\ln(2)}$, $y(4) = 3$.",
   sol:[
    "All solutions: $y = \\int\\frac1{x\\ln(2)}\\,dx = \\log_2(|x|)+C$",
    "$y=3$ when $x=4$: $3 = \\log_2(4)+C = 2+C$, so $C = 1$.",
    "$y(x) = \\log_2(|x|)+1$"],
   ans:"$y = \\log_2|x|+1$"},
  {id:"CN2.4.5-XR14", src:"Course notes 2.4, Exercise 14", lec:"L11", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 78",
   q:"Verify that $P(t) = Ae^{kt}$ is a solution of $\\dfrac{dP}{dt} = kP$ for any real numbers $A$ and $k$.",
   sol:[
    "LHS: $P'(t) = Ae^{kt}\\cdot k = kAe^{kt}$.",
    "RHS: $kP = kAe^{kt}$.",
    "LHS = RHS, so $P(t) = Ae^{kt}$ is a solution."],
   ans:"$P' = kP$ holds for all $A$, $k$", notLectured:true},
  {id:"CN2.4.5-EX13", src:"Course notes 2.4, Example 13", lec:"L11", kind:"example", page:"p. 78",
   q:"A cell has an initial volume of 1350 $\\mu$m$^3$ and 2 hours later has a volume of 4050 $\\mu$m$^3$. Given that the cell is growing exponentially, find a formula for the volume $V$ of the cell at any time $t$ in hours.",
   sol:[
    "Model: $V(t) = ae^{kt}$, with $V(0)=1350$ and $V(2)=4050$.",
    "$1350 = V(0) = ae^0 = a$",
    "$4050 = 1350e^{2k} \\Rightarrow 3 = e^{2k} \\Rightarrow \\ln(3) = 2k \\Rightarrow k = \\frac{\\ln(3)}2$",
    "$V(t) = 1350e^{\\ln(3)t/2}$ $\\mu$m$^3$"],
   ans:"$V(t) = 1350e^{\\ln(3)t/2}$ $\\mu$m$^3$", notLectured:true},
  {id:"CN2.4.5-EX14", src:"Course notes 2.4, Example 14", lec:"L11", kind:"example", page:"p. 79",
   q:"A drug is given intravenously. Right after injection its concentration in the bloodstream is 1.5 mg/ml. After four hours it has dropped to 0.25 mg/ml. Given that the concentration decays exponentially, find a formula for the concentration $C(t)$ at any time $t$ (hours).",
   sol:[
    "Model: $C(t) = ae^{kt}$, with $C(0) = \\frac32$ and $C(4) = \\frac14$.",
    "$\\frac32 = C(0) = a$",
    "$\\frac14 = \\frac32e^{4k} \\Rightarrow \\frac16 = e^{4k} \\Rightarrow \\ln\\left(\\frac16\\right) = 4k \\Rightarrow k = \\frac14\\ln\\left(\\frac16\\right)$",
    "$C(t) = \\frac32e^{\\ln(1/6)t/4}$ mg/ml",
    "Since $0<\\frac16<1$, $\\ln\\left(\\frac16\\right)<0$, so $k<0$ as expected for decay."],
   ans:"$C(t) = \\frac32e^{\\ln(1/6)t/4}$ mg/ml", notLectured:true},
  {id:"CN2.4.5-XR15", src:"Course notes 2.4, Exercise 15", lec:"L11", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 79",
   q:"A population is initially counted at 1000 members and 2 years later it is 1200. Given that the population is growing exponentially, find a formula for the population after $t$ years.",
   sol:[
    "Model: $P(t) = ae^{kt}$, with $P(0)=1000$ and $P(2)=1200$.",
    "$1000 = P(0) = a$",
    "$1200 = 1000e^{2k} \\Rightarrow \\frac65 = e^{2k} \\Rightarrow \\ln\\left(\\frac65\\right) = 2k \\Rightarrow k = \\frac12\\ln\\left(\\frac65\\right)$",
    "$P(t) = 1000e^{t\\ln(6/5)/2}$"],
   ans:"$P(t) = 1000e^{t\\ln(6/5)/2}$", notLectured:true},
  {id:"CN2.4.5-EX15", src:"Course notes 2.4, Example 15", lec:"L11", kind:"example", page:"pp. 80-81",
   q:"When a plant dies, its Carbon-14 decays exponentially with a half-life of 5730 years.<br>(a) A sample now has 10 g of Carbon-14 and had 40 g when it died. How long ago did it die?<br>(b) A different sample had 25 g initially and now has 5 g. How long ago did it die?",
   sol:[
    "(a) The 40 g decays to 20 g in 5730 years, and the 20 g decays to 10 g in another 5730 years.",
    "So the plant died $5730+5730 = 11460$ years ago.",
    "(b) Model: $C(t) = ae^{kt}$ with $C(0) = 25$, so $a = 25$.",
    "Half-life: $C(5730) = \\frac{25}2$, so $\\frac{25}2 = 25e^{5730k} \\Rightarrow \\frac12 = e^{5730k} \\Rightarrow -\\ln(2) = 5730k \\Rightarrow k = -\\frac{\\ln(2)}{5730}$",
    "$C(t) = 25e^{-\\ln(2)t/5730}$. Set $C(t) = 5$:",
    "$e^{-\\ln(2)t/5730} = \\frac15 \\Rightarrow -\\frac{\\ln(2)}{5730}t = -\\ln(5) \\Rightarrow t = \\dfrac{5730\\ln(5)}{\\ln(2)}\\approx 13305$ years"],
   ans:"(a) 11460 years; (b) $\\frac{5730\\ln(5)}{\\ln(2)}\\approx13305$ years",
   note:"In (a) the notes write \"another 5370 years\"; it should be 5730, which is what the sum uses.", notLectured:true},
  {id:"CN2.4.6-EX16", src:"Course notes 2.4, Example 16", lec:"L11", kind:"example", page:"p. 82",
   q:"The Richter scale is a base-10 logarithmic scale of the amplitude of the largest recorded wave at a set distance from an earthquake. How much larger is the amplitude of a magnitude 6 earthquake than a magnitude 3 earthquake?",
   sol:[
    "Let $A_1$ and $A_2$ be the amplitudes. Then $\\log_{10}(A_1) = 6$ and $\\log_{10}(A_2) = 3$.",
    "By the definition of logarithms, $A_1 = 10^6$ and $A_2 = 10^3$.",
    "$\\dfrac{A_1}{A_2} = \\dfrac{10^6}{10^3} = 1000$, so the amplitude is 1000 times larger."],
   ans:"1000 times larger"},
  {id:"CN2.4.6-EX17", src:"Course notes 2.4, Example 17", lec:"L11", kind:"example", page:"p. 82",
   q:"How much larger is the amplitude of a magnitude 4.5 earthquake than a magnitude 2 earthquake?",
   sol:[
    "$\\log_{10}(A_1) = 4.5$ and $\\log_{10}(A_2) = 2$, so $A_1 = 10^{4.5}$ and $A_2 = 10^2$.",
    "$\\dfrac{A_1}{A_2} = \\dfrac{10^{4.5}}{10^2} = 10^{2.5} = 100\\sqrt{10}\\approx 316$"],
   ans:"$10^{2.5}\\approx316$ times larger",
   note:"The notes use $\\sqrt{10}\\approx3.17$ and say about 317 times. In fact $\\sqrt{10}\\approx3.162$, so $10^{2.5}\\approx316$."},
  {id:"CN2.4.6-EX18", src:"Course notes 2.4, Example 18", lec:"L11", kind:"example", page:"p. 83",
   q:"On the decibel scale, a sound of intensity $I$ (W/m$^2$) has loudness $D = 10\\log_{10}\\left(\\dfrac{I}{10^{-12}}\\right)$ decibels. How many times more intense is a sound of $D_1 = 110$ dB than one of $D_2 = 20$ dB?",
   sol:[
    "$110 = 10\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_1}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow 11 = \\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_1}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow 10^{11} = \\frac{I_1}{10^{-12}} \\Rightarrow I_1 = 10^{-1}$",
    "$20 = 10\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_2}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow 2 = \\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_2}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow 10^2 = \\frac{I_2}{10^{-12}} \\Rightarrow I_2 = 10^{-10}$",
    "$\\dfrac{I_1}{I_2} = \\dfrac{10^{-1}}{10^{-10}} = 10^9$"],
   ans:"$10^9$ times as intense"},
  {id:"CN2.4.6-XR16", src:"Course notes 2.4, Exercise 16", lec:"L11", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 83",
   q:"What is the pH of a substance with $[H^+] = 1\\times10^{-6}$ moles per litre?",
   sol:[
    "$\\text{pH} = -\\log_{10}[H^+] = -\\log_{10}(10^{-6}) = -(-6) = 6$"],
   ans:"$6$"},
  {id:"CN2.4.6-XR17", src:"Course notes 2.4, Exercise 17", lec:"L11", kind:"exercise", page:"p. 83",
   q:"How many times louder is a sound of $D_1 = 50$ decibels than a sound of $D_2 = 35$ decibels?",
   sol:[
    "$50 = 10\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_1}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow 5 = \\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_1}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow 10^5 = \\frac{I_1}{10^{-12}} \\Rightarrow I_1 = 10^{-7}$",
    "$35 = 10\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_2}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow 3.5 = \\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_2}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow 10^{3.5} = \\frac{I_2}{10^{-12}} \\Rightarrow I_2 = 10^{-8.5}$",
    "$\\dfrac{I_1}{I_2} = \\dfrac{10^{-7}}{10^{-8.5}} = 10^{1.5}$"],
   ans:"$10^{1.5}\\approx31.6$ times as intense"},
  {id:"CN2.4-P1-a-d", src:"Course notes 2.4, End of Section Problem 1, parts (a) to (d)", lec:"L10", kind:"problem", page:"p. 84",
   q:"Evaluate.<br>(a) $\\log_5(25)$<br>(b) $\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac1{1000}\\right)$<br>(c) $e^{\\ln(7)}$<br>(d) $\\log_2(2^5)$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $\\log_5(25) = 2$ since $5^2 = 25$.",
    "(b) $\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac1{1000}\\right) = -3$ since $10^{-3} = \\frac1{1000}$.",
    "(c) By $e^{\\ln(x)} = x$, $e^{\\ln(7)} = 7$.",
    "(d) $\\log_2(2^5) = 5\\log_2(2) = 5$"],
   ans:"(a) $2$; (b) $-3$; (c) $7$; (d) $5$"},
  {id:"CN2.4-P1-e-h", src:"Course notes 2.4, End of Section Problem 1, parts (e) to (h)", lec:"L10", kind:"problem", page:"p. 84",
   q:"Evaluate.<br>(e) $\\ln\\left(\\frac1e\\right)$<br>(f) $\\log_5(\\sqrt5)$<br>(g) $e^{3\\ln(4)}$<br>(h) $e^{\\ln(7)/2}$",
   sol:[
    "(e) $\\ln\\left(\\frac1e\\right) = \\ln(e^{-1}) = -\\ln(e) = -1$",
    "(f) $\\log_5(\\sqrt5) = \\log_5(5^{1/2}) = \\frac12\\log_5(5) = \\frac12$",
    "(g) $e^{3\\ln(4)} = e^{\\ln(4^3)} = 4^3 = 64$",
    "(h) $e^{\\frac12\\ln(7)} = e^{\\ln(7^{1/2})} = \\sqrt7$"],
   ans:"(e) $-1$; (f) $\\frac12$; (g) $64$; (h) $\\sqrt7$"},
  {id:"CN2.4-P2-a-d", src:"Course notes 2.4, End of Section Problem 2, parts (a) to (d)", lec:"L10", kind:"problem", page:"p. 84",
   q:"Simplify.<br>(a) $x\\cdot(x^2)^3$<br>(b) $2^3\\cdot\\sqrt2$<br>(c) $\\ln(6)-\\ln(2)$<br>(d) $\\log_2(6)-\\log_2(15)+\\log_2(20)$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $x\\cdot(x^2)^3 = x\\cdot x^6 = x^7$",
    "(b) $2^3\\cdot2^{1/2} = 2^{3+1/2} = 2^{7/2}$",
    "(c) $\\ln(6)-\\ln(2) = \\ln\\left(\\frac62\\right) = \\ln(3)$",
    "(d) $\\log_2(6)-\\log_2(15)+\\log_2(20) = \\log_2\\left(\\frac{6\\cdot20}{15}\\right) = \\log_2(8) = \\log_2(2^3) = 3$"],
   ans:"(a) $x^7$; (b) $2^{7/2}$; (c) $\\ln(3)$; (d) $3$"},
  {id:"CN2.4-P2-e-h", src:"Course notes 2.4, End of Section Problem 2, parts (e) to (h)", lec:"L10", kind:"problem", page:"p. 84",
   q:"Simplify.<br>(e) $\\log_{a^2}(a^3)$<br>(f) $(x^n)^3$, $n$ a positive integer<br>(g) $((-1)^nx^n)^2$, $n$ a positive integer<br>(h) $\\dfrac{2^n}{3^{2n}}\\cdot\\dfrac{9^n}{4^n}$, $n$ a positive integer",
   sol:[
    "(e) $a^3 = (a^2)^{3/2}$, so $\\log_{a^2}(a^3) = \\log_{a^2}\\left((a^2)^{3/2}\\right) = \\frac32$",
    "(f) $(x^n)^3 = x^{3n}$",
    "(g) $((-1)^nx^n)^2 = (-1)^{2n}x^{2n} = ((-1)^2)^nx^{2n} = x^{2n}$",
    "(h) $3^{2n} = 9^n$ and $4^n = 2^{2n}$, so $\\dfrac{2^n}{9^n}\\cdot\\dfrac{9^n}{2^{2n}} = \\dfrac{2^n}{2^{2n}} = 2^{-n}$"],
   ans:"(e) $\\frac32$; (f) $x^{3n}$; (g) $x^{2n}$; (h) $2^{-n}$"},
  {id:"CN2.4-P2-i", src:"Course notes 2.4, End of Section Problem 2, part (i)", lec:"L10", kind:"problem", page:"p. 84",
   q:"Simplify.<br>(i) $\\ln(|x|)-\\ln(|x-1|)$",
   sol:[
    "(i) $\\ln(|x|)-\\ln(|x-1|) = \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{|x|}{|x-1|}\\right) = \\ln\\left(\\left|\\dfrac{x}{x-1}\\right|\\right)$"],
   ans:"(i) $\\ln\\left|\\frac{x}{x-1}\\right|$"},
  {id:"CN2.4-P3-a-d", src:"Course notes 2.4, End of Section Problem 3, parts (a) to (d)", lec:"L10", kind:"problem", page:"p. 84",
   q:"Find the inverse of $f$.<br>(a) $f(x) = e^{2x+1}$<br>(b) $f(x) = \\ln(x^2)$<br>(c) $f(x) = \\ln(7-2x)+1$<br>(d) $f(x) = \\dfrac{e^{2x}}2$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $y = e^{2x+1} \\Rightarrow \\ln(y) = 2x+1 \\Rightarrow x = \\frac12(\\ln(y)-1)$",
    "$f^{-1}(y) = \\frac12(\\ln(y)-1)$",
    "(b) For $x>0$, $\\ln(x^2) = 2\\ln(x)$. Then $y = 2\\ln(x) \\Rightarrow \\frac y2 = \\ln(x) \\Rightarrow x = e^{y/2}$.",
    "$f^{-1}(y) = e^{y/2} = \\sqrt{e^y}$ (for $x>0$)",
    "(c) $y-1 = \\ln(7-2x) \\Rightarrow e^{y-1} = 7-2x \\Rightarrow x = \\dfrac{7-e^{y-1}}2$",
    "$f^{-1}(y) = \\dfrac{7-e^{y-1}}2$",
    "(d) $2y = e^{2x} \\Rightarrow \\ln(2y) = 2x \\Rightarrow x = \\frac12\\ln(2y)$",
    "$f^{-1}(y) = \\frac12\\ln(2y)$"],
   ans:"(a) $\\frac12(\\ln(y)-1)$; (b) $e^{y/2}$ (taking $x>0$); (c) $\\frac{7-e^{y-1}}{2}$; (d) $\\frac12\\ln(2y)$",
   note:"In (b) the official solution rewrites $\\ln(x^2)$ as $2\\ln(x)$, which only holds for $x>0$. On its full domain $x\\ne0$, $\\ln(x^2)$ is not one-to-one (for example $f(1)=f(-1)$), so the inverse is taken on $x>0$."},
  {id:"CN2.4-P3-e-g", src:"Course notes 2.4, End of Section Problem 3, parts (e) to (g)", lec:"L10", kind:"problem", page:"p. 84",
   q:"Find the inverse of $f$.<br>(e) $f(x) = \\ln(2+\\ln(x))$<br>(f) $f(t) = 14\\cdot2^t$<br>(g) $f(x) = \\dfrac{e^x-e^{-x}}{e^x+e^{-x}}$",
   sol:[
    "(e) $e^y = 2+\\ln(x) \\Rightarrow e^y-2 = \\ln(x) \\Rightarrow x = e^{e^y-2}$",
    "$f^{-1}(y) = e^{e^y-2}$",
    "(f) $\\frac y{14} = 2^t \\Rightarrow t = \\log_2\\left(\\frac y{14}\\right)$",
    "$f^{-1}(y) = \\log_2\\left(\\dfrac y{14}\\right)$",
    "(g) Multiply top and bottom by $e^x$: $y = \\dfrac{e^{2x}-1}{e^{2x}+1}$",
    "$(e^{2x}+1)y = e^{2x}-1 \\Rightarrow e^{2x}y-e^{2x} = -1-y \\Rightarrow e^{2x}(y-1) = -1-y \\Rightarrow e^{2x} = \\dfrac{-1-y}{y-1}$",
    "$2x = \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{-1-y}{y-1}\\right)$, so $f^{-1}(y) = \\dfrac12\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{-1-y}{y-1}\\right) = \\dfrac12\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{1+y}{1-y}\\right)$"],
   ans:"(e) $e^{e^y-2}$; (f) $\\log_2\\left(\\frac{y}{14}\\right)$; (g) $\\frac12\\ln\\left(\\frac{1+y}{1-y}\\right)$"},
  {id:"CN2.4-P4", src:"Course notes 2.4, End of Section Problem 4", lec:"L10", kind:"problem", page:"p. 84",
   q:"Use the properties of logarithms to write $\\ln(f(x))$ as a sum and difference of functions.<br>(a) $f(x) = \\dfrac{x+2}{x^2(x-1)^3}$<br>(b) $f(x) = \\dfrac{(x-5)^3}{x(x+3)}$<br>(c) $f(x) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt x(x-1)(x+1)}{x+2}$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $\\ln(f(x)) = \\ln(x+2)-\\ln\\left(x^2(x-1)^3\\right) = \\ln(x+2)-\\left[\\ln(x^2)+\\ln\\left((x-1)^3\\right)\\right]$",
    "$= \\ln(x+2)-2\\ln(x)-3\\ln(x-1)$",
    "(b) $\\ln(f(x)) = \\ln\\left((x-5)^3\\right)-[\\ln(x)+\\ln(x+3)]$",
    "$= 3\\ln(x-5)-\\ln(x)-\\ln(x+3)$",
    "(c) $\\ln(f(x)) = \\ln(\\sqrt x)+\\ln(x-1)+\\ln(x+1)-\\ln(x+2)$",
    "$= \\frac12\\ln(x)+\\ln(x-1)+\\ln(x+1)-\\ln(x+2)$"],
   ans:"(a) $\\ln(x+2)-2\\ln(x)-3\\ln(x-1)$; (b) $3\\ln(x-5)-\\ln(x)-\\ln(x+3)$; (c) $\\frac12\\ln(x)+\\ln(x-1)+\\ln(x+1)-\\ln(x+2)$"},
  {id:"CN2.4-P5-a-d", src:"Course notes 2.4, End of Section Problem 5, parts (a) to (d)", lec:"L11", kind:"problem", page:"p. 84",
   q:"Determine the derivative.<br>(a) $f(x) = \\ln(x^2)$<br>(b) $f(x) = e^{\\ln(x)}$<br>(c) $f(x) = x\\ln(x+1)$<br>(d) $f(x) = e^{\\sin(x)}$",
   sol:[
    "(a) By the Chain Rule, $f'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\cdot2x = \\dfrac2x$",
    "(b) $f(x) = e^{\\ln(x)} = x$ (for $x>0$), so $f'(x) = 1$",
    "(c) By the Product Rule, $f'(x) = \\ln(x+1)+x\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{x+1} = \\ln(x+1)+\\dfrac{x}{x+1}$",
    "(d) By the Chain Rule, $f'(x) = e^{\\sin(x)}\\cos(x)$"],
   ans:"(a) $\\frac2x$; (b) $1$; (c) $\\ln(x+1)+\\frac{x}{x+1}$; (d) $e^{\\sin(x)}\\cos(x)$"},
  {id:"CN2.4-P5-e-h", src:"Course notes 2.4, End of Section Problem 5, parts (e) to (h)", lec:"L11", kind:"problem", page:"p. 84",
   q:"Determine the derivative.<br>(e) $f(x) = \\log_2(3x+5)$<br>(f) $f(x) = \\log_3(e^{x^2})$<br>(g) $f(x) = \\dfrac{\\cos(x)-\\ln(x)}x$<br>(h) $f(x) = \\ln(\\ln(x))$",
   sol:[
    "(e) By the Chain Rule, $f'(x) = \\dfrac1{(3x+5)\\ln(2)}\\cdot3 = \\dfrac3{(3x+5)\\ln(2)}$",
    "(f) $f(x) = \\log_3(e^{x^2}) = x^2\\log_3(e)$, so $f'(x) = 2x\\log_3(e)$",
    "(g) By the Quotient Rule, $f'(x) = \\dfrac{\\left(-\\sin(x)-\\frac1x\\right)x-(1)(\\cos(x)-\\ln(x))}{x^2} = \\dfrac{-x\\sin(x)-1-\\cos(x)+\\ln(x)}{x^2}$",
    "(h) By the Chain Rule, $f'(x) = \\dfrac1{\\ln(x)}\\cdot\\dfrac1x = \\dfrac1{x\\ln(x)}$"],
   ans:"(e) $\\frac{3}{(3x+5)\\ln(2)}$; (f) $2x\\log_3(e)$; (g) $\\frac{-x\\sin(x)-1-\\cos(x)+\\ln(x)}{x^2}$; (h) $\\frac{1}{x\\ln(x)}$"},
  {id:"CN2.4-P6", src:"Course notes 2.4, End of Section Problem 6", lec:"L11", kind:"problem", page:"p. 84",
   q:"Verify that the given function $y$ is a solution of the differential equation.<br>(a) $y = 2e^{3t}$ for $\\dfrac{dy}{dt} = 3y$<br>(b) $y = -1+e^{x^2/2}$ for $\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = x(y+1)$<br>(c) $y = -\\dfrac1{\\ln(x)+1}$ for $\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = \\dfrac{y^2}x$<br>(d) $y = |x|$ for $y' = \\dfrac yx$<br>(e) $y = xe^x+e^x$ for $y' = y+e^x$",
   sol:[
    "(a) LHS: $y' = 6e^{3t}$. RHS: $3y = 3(2e^{3t}) = 6e^{3t}$.",
    "LHS = RHS, so it is a solution.",
    "(b) LHS: $y' = e^{x^2/2}\\cdot\\frac{2x}2 = xe^{x^2/2}$. RHS: $x(y+1) = x(-1+e^{x^2/2}+1) = xe^{x^2/2}$.",
    "LHS = RHS, so it is a solution.",
    "(c) $y = -(\\ln(x)+1)^{-1}$, so LHS: $y' = (\\ln(x)+1)^{-2}\\cdot\\frac1x = \\dfrac{1}{x(\\ln(x)+1)^2}$.",
    "RHS: $\\dfrac{y^2}x = \\dfrac{(\\ln(x)+1)^{-2}}x = \\dfrac{1}{x(\\ln(x)+1)^2}$.",
    "LHS = RHS, so it is a solution.",
    "(d) LHS: $y' = \\dfrac{x}{|x|}$. RHS: $\\dfrac yx = \\dfrac{|x|}x = \\dfrac{x}{|x|}$ (Problem 2.1.12).",
    "LHS = RHS (for $x\\ne0$), so it is a solution.",
    "(e) LHS: $y' = e^x+xe^x+e^x = xe^x+2e^x$. RHS: $y+e^x = xe^x+e^x+e^x = xe^x+2e^x$.",
    "LHS = RHS, so it is a solution."],
   ans:"(a) solution; (b) solution; (c) solution; (d) solution; (e) solution"},
  {id:"CN2.4-P7", src:"Course notes 2.4, End of Section Problem 7", lec:"L11", kind:"problem", page:"p. 84",
   q:"Solve the initial value problem.<br>(a) $y' = \\dfrac1x$, $y(-3) = 1$<br>(b) $y' = e^x+x$, $y(1) = 2$<br>(c) $y' = \\dfrac1{x\\ln(3)}$, $y(3) = 3$<br>(d) $y' = \\dfrac3x$, $y(-2) = 5$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $y = \\int\\frac1x\\,dx = \\ln(|x|)+C$",
    "$y=1$ when $x=-3$: $1 = \\ln(|-3|)+C$, so $C = 1-\\ln(3)$.",
    "$y = \\ln(|x|)+1-\\ln(3)$",
    "(b) $y = \\int(e^x+x)\\,dx = e^x+\\frac12x^2+C$",
    "$y=2$ when $x=1$: $2 = e+\\frac12+C$, so $C = \\frac32-e$.",
    "$y = e^x+\\frac{x^2}2-e+\\frac32$",
    "(c) $y = \\int\\frac1{x\\ln(3)}\\,dx = \\log_3(|x|)+C$",
    "$y=3$ when $x=3$: $3 = \\log_3(3)+C = 1+C$, so $C = 2$.",
    "$y = \\log_3(|x|)+2$",
    "(d) $y = \\int\\frac3x\\,dx = 3\\ln(|x|)+C$",
    "$y=5$ when $x=-2$: $5 = 3\\ln(2)+C = \\ln(8)+C$, so $C = 5-\\ln(8)$.",
    "$y = 3\\ln(|x|)+5-\\ln(8)$"],
   ans:"(a) $y=\\ln|x|+1-\\ln(3)$; (b) $y=e^x+\\frac{x^2}{2}-e+\\frac32$; (c) $y=\\log_3|x|+2$; (d) $y=3\\ln|x|+5-\\ln(8)$"},
  {id:"CN2.4-P8", src:"Course notes 2.4, End of Section Problem 8", lec:"L10", kind:"problem", page:"p. 84",
   q:"Sketch the graph of $y = f(x)$ and state the domain and range of $f$.<br>(a) $f(x) = e^{-x+1}$<br>(b) $f(x) = e^{x+1}-1$<br>(c) $f(x) = \\ln(x-1)$<br>(d) $f(x) = |\\ln(x+1)|$<br>(e) $f(x) = 2e^{x-2}+2$",
   sol:[
    "(a) Write $f(x) = e^{-(x-1)}$: start with the graph of $e^{-x}$ and shift it 1 unit right.",
    "Domain $(-\\infty,\\infty)$, range $(0,\\infty)$.",
    "(b) Start with $e^x$, shift 1 unit left, then 1 unit down (horizontal asymptote $y=-1$).",
    "Domain $(-\\infty,\\infty)$, range $(-1,\\infty)$.",
    "(c) Start with $\\ln(x)$ and shift 1 unit right (vertical asymptote $x=1$).",
    "Domain $x>1$, range $(-\\infty,\\infty)$.",
    "(d) Start with $\\ln(x)$, reflect the part with $0<x<1$ (where $\\ln(x)<0$) above the axis, then shift 1 unit left.",
    "Domain $x>-1$, range $[0,\\infty)$.",
    "(e) Start with $e^x$, shift 2 units right, stretch vertically by a factor of 2, then shift 2 units up (horizontal asymptote $y=2$).",
    "Domain $(-\\infty,\\infty)$, range $(2,\\infty)$."],
   ans:"(a) domain $(-\\infty,\\infty)$, range $(0,\\infty)$; (b) domain $(-\\infty,\\infty)$, range $(-1,\\infty)$; (c) domain $(1,\\infty)$, range $(-\\infty,\\infty)$; (d) domain $(-1,\\infty)$, range $[0,\\infty)$; (e) domain $(-\\infty,\\infty)$, range $(2,\\infty)$"},
  {id:"CN2.4-P9", src:"Course notes 2.4, End of Section Problem 9", lec:"L10", kind:"problem", page:"p. 84",
   q:"Use the definition $\\ln(x) = \\displaystyle\\int_1^x\\frac1t\\,dt$ and the left Riemann sum with 4 subdivisions to approximate the following.<br>(a) $\\ln(3)$<br>(b) $\\ln(5)$<br>(c) $\\ln(7)$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $\\ln(3) = \\int_1^3\\frac1t\\,dt$. $\\Delta t = \\frac{3-1}4 = \\frac12$, $t_i = 1+\\frac i2$.",
    "$\\ln(3)\\approx\\frac11\\cdot\\frac12+\\frac1{3/2}\\cdot\\frac12+\\frac12\\cdot\\frac12+\\frac1{5/2}\\cdot\\frac12 = \\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac14+\\frac15 = \\frac{77}{60}$",
    "(b) $\\ln(5) = \\int_1^5\\frac1t\\,dt$. $\\Delta t = \\frac{5-1}4 = 1$, $t_i = 1+i$.",
    "$\\ln(5)\\approx\\frac11(1)+\\frac12(1)+\\frac13(1)+\\frac14(1) = \\frac{25}{12}$",
    "(c) $\\ln(7) = \\int_1^7\\frac1t\\,dt$. $\\Delta t = \\frac{7-1}4 = \\frac32$, $t_i = 1+\\frac{3i}2$, so $t_i = 1, \\frac52, 4, \\frac{11}2$.",
    "$\\ln(7)\\approx\\frac11\\cdot\\frac32+\\frac25\\cdot\\frac32+\\frac14\\cdot\\frac32+\\frac2{11}\\cdot\\frac32 = \\frac32+\\frac35+\\frac38+\\frac3{11} = \\frac{1209}{440}$"],
   ans:"(a) $\\frac{77}{60}$; (b) $\\frac{25}{12}$; (c) $\\frac{1209}{440}$"},
  {id:"CN2.4-P10", src:"Course notes 2.4, End of Section Problem 10", lec:"L11", kind:"problem", page:"p. 85",
   q:"Find the pH of a substance with the given concentration $[H^+]$ (moles per litre).<br>(a) $1\\times10^{-2}$<br>(b) $1\\times10^{-7}$<br>(c) $1\\times10^{-3.5}$<br>(d) $1\\times10^{-4.1}$",
   sol:[
    "(a) $\\text{pH} = -\\log_{10}(10^{-2}) = -(-2) = 2$",
    "(b) $\\text{pH} = -\\log_{10}(10^{-7}) = 7$",
    "(c) $\\text{pH} = -\\log_{10}(10^{-3.5}) = 3.5$",
    "(d) $\\text{pH} = -\\log_{10}(10^{-4.1}) = 4.1$"],
   ans:"(a) $2$; (b) $7$; (c) $3.5$; (d) $4.1$"},
  {id:"CN2.4-P11", src:"Course notes 2.4, End of Section Problem 11", lec:"L11", kind:"problem", page:"p. 85",
   q:"Show mathematically (as in Example 16) that the amplitude of a magnitude 8 earthquake is 10000 times larger than that of a magnitude 4 earthquake.",
   sol:[
    "$\\log_{10}(A_1) = 8$, so $A_1 = 10^8$. $\\log_{10}(A_2) = 4$, so $A_2 = 10^4$.",
    "$\\dfrac{A_1}{A_2} = \\dfrac{10^8}{10^4} = 10^4 = 10000$"],
   ans:"$\\frac{A_1}{A_2} = 10^4 = 10000$"},
  {id:"CN2.4-P12", src:"Course notes 2.4, End of Section Problem 12", lec:"L11", kind:"problem", page:"p. 85",
   q:"Show mathematically (as in Example 16) that the amplitude of a magnitude 2 earthquake is $\\frac1{100}$ times that of a magnitude 4 earthquake.",
   sol:[
    "$\\log_{10}(A_1) = 2$, so $A_1 = 10^2$. $\\log_{10}(A_2) = 4$, so $A_2 = 10^4$.",
    "$\\dfrac{A_1}{A_2} = \\dfrac{10^2}{10^4} = 10^{-2} = \\dfrac1{100}$"],
   ans:"$\\frac{A_1}{A_2} = \\frac{1}{100}$"},
  {id:"CN2.4-P13", src:"Course notes 2.4, End of Section Problem 13", lec:"L11", kind:"problem", page:"p. 85",
   q:"Find the intensity of each sound (as in Example 18) and use it to determine how many times louder $D_1$ is than $D_2$.<br>(a) $D_1 = 40$ dB, $D_2 = 30$ dB<br>(b) $D_1 = 60$ dB, $D_2 = 20$ dB<br>(c) $D_1 = 95$ dB, $D_2 = 35$ dB<br>(d) $D_1 = 50$ dB, $D_2 = 45$ dB",
   sol:[
    "(a) $40 = 10\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_1}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow 4 = \\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_1}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow 10^{4} = \\frac{I_1}{10^{-12}} \\Rightarrow I_1 = 10^{-8}$",
    "$30 = 10\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_2}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow 3 = \\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_2}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow 10^{3} = \\frac{I_2}{10^{-12}} \\Rightarrow I_2 = 10^{-9}$",
    "$\\dfrac{I_1}{I_2} = \\dfrac{10^{-8}}{10^{-9}} = 10$",
    "(b) $60 = 10\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_1}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow 6 = \\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_1}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow 10^{6} = \\frac{I_1}{10^{-12}} \\Rightarrow I_1 = 10^{-6}$",
    "$20 = 10\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_2}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow 2 = \\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_2}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow 10^{2} = \\frac{I_2}{10^{-12}} \\Rightarrow I_2 = 10^{-10}$",
    "$\\dfrac{I_1}{I_2} = \\dfrac{10^{-6}}{10^{-10}} = 10^4$",
    "(c) $95 = 10\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_1}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow 9.5 = \\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_1}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow 10^{9.5} = \\frac{I_1}{10^{-12}} \\Rightarrow I_1 = 10^{-2.5}$",
    "$35 = 10\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_2}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow 3.5 = \\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_2}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow 10^{3.5} = \\frac{I_2}{10^{-12}} \\Rightarrow I_2 = 10^{-8.5}$",
    "$\\dfrac{I_1}{I_2} = \\dfrac{10^{-2.5}}{10^{-8.5}} = 10^6$",
    "(d) $50 = 10\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_1}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow 5 = \\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_1}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow 10^{5} = \\frac{I_1}{10^{-12}} \\Rightarrow I_1 = 10^{-7}$",
    "$45 = 10\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_2}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow 4.5 = \\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{I_2}{10^{-12}}\\right) \\Rightarrow 10^{4.5} = \\frac{I_2}{10^{-12}} \\Rightarrow I_2 = 10^{-7.5}$",
    "$\\dfrac{I_1}{I_2} = \\dfrac{10^{-7}}{10^{-7.5}} = 10^{1/2}\\approx3.16$"],
   ans:"(a) $10$ times; (b) $10^4$ times; (c) $10^6$ times; (d) $10^{1/2}$ times"},
  {id:"CN2.4-P14", src:"Course notes 2.4, End of Section Problem 14", lec:"L11", kind:"problem", page:"p. 85",
   q:"A culture of bacteria has an initial population of 100 and a doubling time of 3 hours. What will the population be after 9 hours?",
   sol:[
    "The population doubles every 3 hours: 200 after 3 hours, 400 after 6 hours, 800 after 9 hours.",
    "(Equivalently $P(t) = 100\\cdot2^{t/3}$, so $P(9) = 100\\cdot2^3 = 800$.)"],
   ans:"$800$", notLectured:true},
  {id:"CN2.4-P15", src:"Course notes 2.4, End of Section Problem 15", lec:"L11", kind:"problem", page:"p. 85",
   q:"A biodome is initially home to 500 birds. After 1 year the population doubles to 1000 birds.<br>(a) Write $P(t)$ using the natural exponential function.<br>(b) Write $P(t)$ using an exponential function with base 2.<br>(c) Assuming exponential growth, how long until the population reaches 4000?<br>(d) How long until it reaches 2500?",
   sol:[
    "(a) $P(t) = ae^{kt}$ with $P(0)=500$ and $P(1)=1000$. $500 = ae^0 = a$. $1000 = 500e^{k} \\Rightarrow 2 = e^k \\Rightarrow k = \\ln(2)$.",
    "$P(t) = 500e^{\\ln(2)t}$",
    "(b) $P(t) = a2^{kt}$. $500 = a2^0 = a$. $1000 = 500\\cdot2^{k} \\Rightarrow 2 = 2^k \\Rightarrow k = 1$. So $P(t) = 500\\cdot2^t$.",
    "(c) $4000 = 500\\cdot2^t \\Rightarrow 8 = 2^t \\Rightarrow t = \\log_2(8) = 3$ years. (Or double: $500\\to1000\\to2000\\to4000$.)",
    "(d) $2500 = 500e^{\\ln(2)t} \\Rightarrow 5 = e^{\\ln(2)t} \\Rightarrow \\ln(5) = \\ln(2)t \\Rightarrow t = \\dfrac{\\ln(5)}{\\ln(2)}\\approx2.32$ years"],
   ans:"(a) $500e^{\\ln(2)t}$; (b) $500\\cdot2^t$; (c) 3 years; (d) $\\frac{\\ln 5}{\\ln 2}$ years", notLectured:true},
  {id:"CN2.4-P16", src:"Course notes 2.4, End of Section Problem 16", lec:"L11", kind:"problem", page:"p. 85",
   q:"A culture of Salmonella enteritidis initially contains 50 cells. In a nutrient broth it grows at a rate proportional to its size. After 2 hours the population has increased to 1000. Find the number of bacteria after 3 hours. After how many hours will the population reach 250 000?",
   sol:[
    "$\\frac{dP}{dt} = kP$, so $P(t) = ae^{kt}$, with $P(0) = 50$ and $P(2) = 1000$.",
    "$50 = ae^0 = a$. $1000 = 50e^{2k} \\Rightarrow 20 = e^{2k} \\Rightarrow k = \\frac12\\ln(20)$.",
    "$P(t) = 50e^{\\ln(20)t/2}$",
    "After 3 hours: $P(3) = 50e^{3\\ln(20)/2} = 50\\cdot20^{3/2}\\approx4472$ bacteria.",
    "$250000 = 50e^{\\ln(20)t/2} \\Rightarrow 5000 = e^{\\ln(20)t/2} \\Rightarrow \\ln(5000) = \\frac12\\ln(20)t \\Rightarrow t = \\dfrac{2\\ln(5000)}{\\ln(20)}\\approx5.69$ hours"],
   ans:"$P(3) = 50e^{3\\ln(20)/2}\\approx4472$; $t = \\frac{2\\ln(5000)}{\\ln(20)}\\approx5.69$ h", notLectured:true},
  {id:"CN2.4-P17", src:"Course notes 2.4, End of Section Problem 17", lec:"L11", kind:"problem", page:"p. 85",
   q:"The half-life of $^{14}$C is 5730 years. When an organism dies, $^{14}$C starts to decay. A piece of parchment has 90% of the $^{14}$C content of paper today. What is the age of the artifact?",
   sol:[
    "$C(t) = ae^{kt}$. Half-life: $C(5730) = \\frac12C(0)$, so $e^{5730k} = \\frac12 \\Rightarrow 5730k = \\ln\\left(\\frac12\\right) \\Rightarrow k = -\\frac{\\ln(2)}{5730}$.",
    "The age $t_0$ satisfies $C(t_0) = 0.9\\,C(0)$: $0.9a = ae^{-\\ln(2)t_0/5730} \\Rightarrow \\ln(0.9) = -\\frac{\\ln(2)}{5730}t_0$",
    "$t_0 = \\dfrac{-5730\\ln(0.9)}{\\ln(2)}\\approx871$ years"],
   ans:"$\\frac{-5730\\ln(0.9)}{\\ln(2)}\\approx871$ years", notLectured:true},
  {id:"CN2.4-P18", src:"Course notes 2.4, End of Section Problem 18", lec:"L11", kind:"problem", page:"p. 85",
   q:"A substance has a half-life of 16 years and there is initially 24 mg. Determine a model for the amount of substance at any time $t$ (in years). How many years until there is 2 mg remaining?",
   sol:[
    "$S(t) = ae^{kt}$ with $S(0) = 24$ and $S(16) = 12$. $24 = ae^0 = a$.",
    "$12 = 24e^{16k} \\Rightarrow \\frac12 = e^{16k} \\Rightarrow -\\ln(2) = 16k \\Rightarrow k = -\\frac{\\ln(2)}{16}$",
    "$S(t) = 24e^{-\\ln(2)t/16}$",
    "$2 = 24e^{-\\ln(2)t/16} \\Rightarrow \\frac1{12} = e^{-\\ln(2)t/16} \\Rightarrow -\\ln(12) = -\\frac{\\ln(2)}{16}t \\Rightarrow t = \\dfrac{16\\ln(12)}{\\ln(2)}\\approx57.4$ years"],
   ans:"$S(t) = 24e^{-\\ln(2)t/16}$; $t = \\frac{16\\ln(12)}{\\ln(2)}\\approx57.4$ years", notLectured:true},
  {id:"CN2.4-P19", src:"Course notes 2.4, End of Section Problem 19", lec:"L11", kind:"problem", page:"p. 85",
   q:"The half-life of morphine in the bloodstream is 3 hours, and it decays at a rate proportional to the amount present.<br>(a) Write a differential equation for the amount of morphine in the bloodstream.<br>(b) If there is initially 0.4 mg of morphine, how long until only 0.01 mg remains?",
   sol:[
    "(a) Let $M(t)$ be the amount after $t$ hours. Decay proportional to the amount means $\\frac{dM}{dt} = kM$, which gives $M(t) = ae^{kt}$.",
    "Half-life 3 hours: $\\frac12a = ae^{3k} \\Rightarrow \\frac12 = e^{3k} \\Rightarrow k = -\\frac{\\ln(2)}3$. So $\\dfrac{dM}{dt} = -\\dfrac{\\ln(2)}3M$.",
    "(b) $M(0) = 0.4$, so $a = 0.4$. Set $M(t) = 0.01$:",
    "$0.01 = 0.4e^{-\\ln(2)t/3} \\Rightarrow \\frac1{40} = e^{-\\ln(2)t/3} \\Rightarrow -\\ln(40) = -\\frac{\\ln(2)}3t \\Rightarrow t = \\dfrac{3\\ln(40)}{\\ln(2)}\\approx15.97$ hours"],
   ans:"(a) $\\frac{dM}{dt} = -\\frac{\\ln(2)}{3}M$; (b) $\\frac{3\\ln(40)}{\\ln(2)}\\approx16$ h", notLectured:true},
  {id:"CN2.4-P20", src:"Course notes 2.4, End of Section Problem 20", lec:"L10", kind:"problem", page:"p. 85",
   q:"A bacteria population is 2500 at time $t=0$ and its growth rate is $1200e^{2t}$ bacteria per hour after $t$ hours.<br>(a) What is the population after 2 hours?<br>(b) What is the population after $x$ hours?",
   sol:[
    "We are given the rate of change of $P$, so the net change is an integral. An antiderivative of $1200e^{2t}$ is $F(t) = 600e^{2t}$ (check: $F'(t) = 1200e^{2t}$).",
    "(a) $\\Delta P = \\int_0^2 1200e^{2t}\\,dt = F(2)-F(0) = 600e^4-600$",
    "$P(2) = 2500+\\Delta P = 2500+600e^4-600 = 1900+600e^4$",
    "(b) $\\Delta P = \\int_0^x1200e^{2t}\\,dt = 600e^{2x}-600$, so $P(x) = 2500+600e^{2x}-600 = 1900+600e^{2x}$"],
   ans:"(a) $1900+600e^4$; (b) $1900+600e^{2x}$",
   note:"Uses the reverse chain rule, which the lectures had not covered at this point."},
  {id:"CN2.4-P21", src:"Course notes 2.4, End of Section Problem 21", lec:"L10", kind:"problem", page:"p. 85",
   q:"A coloured dye is injected into the heart at time $t=0$ at a constant rate $R>0$ and mixes with the blood. The diluted mixture flows out at a constant rate $r>0$. The heart has constant volume $V>0$, and the concentration of dye satisfies $\\dfrac{dC}{dt} = \\dfrac RV-\\dfrac rVC(t)$.<br>(a) Verify that $C(t) = \\dfrac Rr\\left(1-e^{-(r/V)t}\\right)$ is a solution.<br>(b) Find $C^{-1}$ and describe what it calculates.",
   sol:[
    "(a) $C(t) = \\frac Rr-\\frac Rre^{-tr/V}$, so $C'(t) = 0-\\frac Rre^{-tr/V}\\left(-\\frac rV\\right) = \\frac RVe^{-tr/V}$.",
    "RHS: $\\frac RV-\\frac rV\\left(\\frac Rr-\\frac Rre^{-tr/V}\\right) = \\frac RV-\\frac RV+\\frac RVe^{-tr/V} = \\frac RVe^{-tr/V} = C'(t)$, as required.",
    "(b) Let $y = \\frac Rr-\\frac Rre^{-tr/V}$. Then $\\frac Rr-y = \\frac Rre^{-tr/V}$, so $1-\\frac rRy = e^{-tr/V}$.",
    "$\\ln\\left(1-\\frac rRy\\right) = -\\frac{tr}V$, so $t = -\\frac Vr\\ln\\left(1-\\frac rRy\\right)$.",
    "$C^{-1}(y) = -\\dfrac Vr\\ln\\left(1-\\dfrac rRy\\right)$. It gives the time at which the concentration of dye in the heart is $y$."],
   ans:"(b) $C^{-1}(y) = -\\frac{V}{r}\\ln\\left(1-\\frac{r}{R}y\\right)$, the time when the concentration is $y$"}
]);


